window.__DIYDB__=window.__DIYDB__||{data:{}};window.__DIYDB__.data["4runner"]=[{"id":"4runner-front-bumper","title":"Steel Front Bumper for the 5th Gen 4Runner","vehicle":"4runner","category":"armor","type":"part","difficulty":3,"time_hours":{"low":2,"high":4},"cost_usd":{"low":849,"high":1799},"tools_required":["socket set","torque wrench","floor jack","jack stands","drill and bits","wire harness tools"],"skills_required":["basic wrenching","light electrical for sensor and fog light reinstall"],"build_tiers":["mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2010-2024"],"tags":["armor","front-bumper","steel","winch-mount","approach-angle","parking-sensor"],"parts":[{"name":"ARB Summit Bar Front Bumper (5th Gen 4Runner)","vendor":"ARB 4x4 Accessories","price_estimate_usd":1699},{"name":"CBI Offroad Front Bumper (5th Gen 4Runner 2010-2024)","vendor":"CBI Offroad","price_estimate_usd":1299},{"name":"Expedition One 4Runner Front Bumper (5th Gen)","vendor":"Expedition One","price_estimate_usd":1399},{"name":"Fab Four 4Runner Winch Bumper (5th Gen)","vendor":"Fab Four","price_estimate_usd":1449}],"citations":[{"title":"ARB Summit Bar 4Runner Product Page","url":"https://www.arbusa.com"},{"title":"CBI Offroad 4Runner Bumper Product Page","url":"https://www.cbioffroad.com"}],"alternatives":["4runner-winch","4runner-sliders","4runner-skid-plates"],"summary":"The 5th gen 4Runner's factory bumper sits low with a full-width plastic chin that reduces approach angle \u2014 a steel replacement improves the geometry, mounts a winch properly, and adds the recovery infrastructure the factory design lacks.","description":"The ARB Summit Bar is the premium choice for the 4Runner and the most commonly specified bumper on serious build threads. It's full-width, designed to accept a Warn or Mile Marker winch in the integrated plate, and includes mounting provisions for OEM fog lights and parking sensors. The weld and finish quality is class-leading at this price point. It is not a light bumper \u2014 the Summit Bar adds approximately 80\u2013100 lbs \u2014 but on a 4Runner that is carrying expedition gear, the additional mass is already in the budget.\n\nParking sensors are present on most 5th gen 4Runner trims from 2010 onward and the position varies by trim year. Most quality bumpers include sensor relocation brackets and drilling templates. Confirm your specific trim's sensor configuration (number of sensors and position) against the bumper manufacturer's compatibility chart before ordering. CBI, ARB, Expedition One, and Fab Four all offer sensor-compatible designs \u2014 some require minor drilling to accept the OEM sensor housing, which takes 20 minutes and is not an obstacle.\n\nFull-width vs stubby is an approach-angle trade-off. Full-width bumpers protect the outer fender edges on brush and tight trail situations. Stubby bumpers (used by some Fab Four designs) improve the approach angle by removing the outer wing sections \u2014 the result is 3\u00b0\u20135\u00b0 more approach clearance at the cost of the outer fender protection. For rock crawling, stubby is often preferred. For overland and mixed use, full-width is more practical.\n\nThe improvement in approach angle from any of these bumpers over the OEM unit is roughly 5\u00b0\u20137\u00b0 on the 4Runner. The OEM bumper's low chin catches on exit lips and ledge faces that an aftermarket bumper clears. That's a tangible difference on moderate rocky terrain.","steps":["Confirm trim and parking sensor configuration \u2014 check driver's manual or count OEM sensors in factory bumper","Order bumper with appropriate sensor kit for your trim","Disconnect battery negative terminal","Remove OEM bumper: typically 6\u20138 bolts along top edge plus lower support brackets","Remove fog lights and wiring harness from OEM bumper; set aside","Remove parking sensors from OEM bumper; transfer to new bumper using provided drilling template","Transfer fog lights into new bumper housing","Test-fit new bumper with all wiring in place before torquing any hardware","Bolt bumper to frame using supplied hardware; torque to spec (typically 70\u201390 ft-lb)","Reconnect all lighting and sensor wiring; reconnect battery","Test all lighting, sensors, and camera function before closing up","If installing winch: complete winch installation on bench before bumper install \u2014 easier to torque winch mounting bolts before bumper is on the truck"],"pros":["ARB Summit Bar is the benchmark 4Runner bumper \u2014 excellent quality, proven fit, wide accessory ecosystem","Integrated winch plate provides the proper load path for 10,000+ lb winch pull","Approach angle improvement of 5\u00b0\u20137\u00b0 is meaningful on technical rocky terrain","D-ring recovery tabs rated to 4.75 tons on ARB designs \u2014 correct for trail recovery use","Parking sensor compatibility built into most quality designs \u2014 no need for DIY bracket fabrication"],"cons":["ARB Summit Bar is the most expensive option at $1,699 \u2014 verify the build justifies the premium vs CBI at $1,299","Full-width designs add 80\u2013100 lbs to the front of the truck","Solo install is manageable but a second person makes alignment and bolt starting much easier","Parking sensor relocation may require software recalibration on some 4Runner trims \u2014 check with dealer","Stubby bumper designs sacrifice outer fender protection that matters on brushy trails"]},{"id":"4runner-rear-bumper","title":"Rear Bumper and Swing-Out Tire Carrier for the 4Runner","vehicle":"4runner","category":"armor","type":"part","difficulty":3,"time_hours":{"low":2,"high":3},"cost_usd":{"low":899,"high":1799},"tools_required":["socket set","torque wrench","floor jack","jack stands","drill and bits","wire harness tools"],"skills_required":["basic wrenching","light electrical for backup camera and lights"],"build_tiers":["mild_trail","moderate_trail","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2010-2024"],"tags":["armor","rear-bumper","swing-out","tire-carrier","departure-angle","backup-camera"],"parts":[{"name":"CBI Offroad 4Runner Rear Bumper (5th Gen 2010-2024)","vendor":"CBI Offroad","price_estimate_usd":899},{"name":"Relentless Fabrication 4Runner Rear Bumper with Swing-Out","vendor":"Relentless Fabrication","price_estimate_usd":1099},{"name":"Addictive Desert Designs 4Runner Rear Bumper","vendor":"Addictive Desert Designs","price_estimate_usd":1199},{"name":"Ranch Hand Legend 4Runner Rear Bumper","vendor":"Ranch Hand","price_estimate_usd":849}],"citations":[{"title":"CBI Offroad 4Runner Rear Bumper Product Page","url":"https://www.cbioffroad.com"},{"title":"Relentless Fabrication 4Runner Products","url":"https://www.relentlessfabrication.com"}],"alternatives":["4runner-recovery-gear-kit","4runner-sliders"],"summary":"A steel rear bumper on the 4Runner improves departure angle, adds proper recovery points, and \u2014 when combined with a swing-out carrier \u2014 moves the spare tire off the interior floor and frees the cargo area entirely.","description":"The 5th gen 4Runner carries its spare tire under the rear cargo floor, consuming significant interior space and adding weight above the cargo line. An aftermarket rear bumper with an integrated swing-out tire carrier moves the spare outside the vehicle, mounts it on a hinged arm that swings clear of the rear gate, and reclaims that floor space for gear. For overland rigs where the back of the 4Runner is a living space or gear storage system, this is a meaningful quality-of-life upgrade alongside the trail protection benefits.\n\nDeparture angle improvement is 3\u00b0\u20135\u00b0 over the OEM bumper. The factory rear bumper hangs lower than a steel replacement and has rounded corners that snag on exit obstacles. A straight-cut or notched steel bumper rides higher on the frame and presents a cleaner exit profile. The 4Runner's IFS-front and live-axle-rear combination makes departure angle more of a real-world constraint than approach angle on most trail scenarios \u2014 a high departure angle protects the bumper and rear skid from rock strikes as the truck exits ledges and steep transitions.\n\nSwing-out tire carriers for 33\" tires are bolt-on on most designs. Carriers for 35\" tires require more robust pivot hardware and often a reinforced bumper base \u2014 verify the carrier's rated tire diameter before ordering. Backup camera retention varies by bumper design; most quality bumpers include a camera relocation mount. Confirm camera mount compatibility for your 4Runner's specific camera type (OEM housing shape varies by year).\n\nRanch Hand is a budget-tier steel bumper option \u2014 the quality is adequate for overland use, but the design is less refined and departure angle improvement is more modest than CBI or Relentless designs. It's a usable option for builds where budget is the primary constraint.","steps":["Measure swing-out arc clearance if planning a tire carrier \u2014 verify the carrier clears the body when fully opened","Disconnect battery negative terminal","Remove OEM rear bumper: typically 4\u20136 bolts plus lower support brackets and wiring","Transfer trailer wiring connector from OEM to new bumper","Transfer backup camera from OEM bumper housing to new bumper camera mount","Test-fit new bumper before attaching wiring","Bolt bumper to frame; torque to spec","Reconnect trailer wiring and backup camera; test all functions","Install swing-out carrier hardware if applicable; mount spare tire and verify swing clearance at full open","Reconnect battery; verify all rear lights and camera"],"pros":["Swing-out carrier moves spare tire out of the cargo area \u2014 reclaims significant interior space on the 4Runner","3\u00b0\u20135\u00b0 departure angle improvement over OEM","Rated D-ring recovery tabs for rear vehicle recovery","Backup camera provisions on most quality designs","Relentless Fabrication swing-out designs are well-regarded for pivot quality and long-term use with a loaded carrier"],"cons":["Swing-out carriers carrying a 33\"+ spare add substantial cantilevered weight \u2014 torque pivot bolts to spec and recheck annually","Backup camera relocation may change viewing angle slightly; may need adjustment in camera settings","Ranch Hand is the budget option \u2014 adequate but less departure angle improvement than premium designs","Solo install is manageable; having a second person to hold the bumper while starting frame bolts saves significant frustration","Some swing-out designs add 80\u2013120 lbs total with the spare \u2014 rear suspension may need to account for this added weight"]},{"id":"4runner-skid-plates","title":"Skid Plate Kit for the 5th Gen 4Runner","vehicle":"4runner","category":"armor","type":"kit","difficulty":2,"time_hours":{"low":2,"high":4},"cost_usd":{"low":549,"high":999},"tools_required":["floor jack","jack stands","socket set","torque wrench","drill and bits"],"skills_required":["basic wrenching","underbody work"],"build_tiers":["mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler"],"year_compatibility":["2010-2024"],"tags":["armor","skid-plate","underbody-protection","engine-skid","transfer-case","4runner"],"parts":[{"name":"ASFIR 4Runner Full Underbody Skid Kit (5th Gen)","vendor":"ASFIR","price_estimate_usd":849},{"name":"CBI Offroad 4Runner Skid Plate Kit (5th Gen 2010-2024)","vendor":"CBI Offroad","price_estimate_usd":749},{"name":"Slick Rock Fabrications 4Runner Skid Plate System (5th Gen)","vendor":"Slick Rock Fabrications","price_estimate_usd":599},{"name":"Rock Slide Engineering 4Runner Skid Plates (5th Gen)","vendor":"Rock Slide Engineering","price_estimate_usd":699}],"citations":[{"title":"ASFIR 4Runner Products","url":"https://www.asfir4x4.com"},{"title":"CBI Offroad 4Runner Skid Plate Product Page","url":"https://www.cbioffroad.com"}],"alternatives":["4runner-front-bumper","4runner-sliders"],"summary":"Trail Edition and TRD Pro 4Runners come with factory skid plates \u2014 but they're thin stamped steel that covers the engine only. Aftermarket full skid systems extend that protection to the transfer case, fuel tank, and rear differential with heavier material that can take a real hit.","description":"The 5th gen 4Runner Trail Edition and TRD Pro trims include factory aluminum skid plates on the engine and front underbody. SR5 and Limited trims have no factory skids at all. In either case, aftermarket full skid kits improve coverage and material thickness. The factory plates are 3mm aluminum and sized only to protect the oil pan and front crossmember \u2014 they won't handle a direct rock hit on the transfer case or a high-centering event on the fuel tank.\n\nASFIR's full kit is the most comprehensive option \u2014 it covers the front crossmember, oil pan, transmission, transfer case, fuel tank, and rear differential in 3/16\" steel. That's the deepest coverage available for the 5th gen. CBI Offroad's kit uses similar coverage with slightly lighter materials and a lower price point. Slick Rock Fabrications offers a steel kit at the most accessible price but with somewhat lighter gauge material \u2014 suitable for mild to moderate trail use.\n\nThe 4Runner's independent front suspension means the front skid attaches to the frame and subframe, not the lower control arms as on solid-axle vehicles. This matters because the skid must flex slightly relative to the suspension travel on IFS designs \u2014 most aftermarket kits account for this with slotted mounting holes or flexible bracket designs. Verify that the skid you're ordering is designed specifically for the 5th gen IFS architecture.\n\nThe fuel tank skid is particularly important on the 4Runner. The factory fuel tank on the 5th gen hangs relatively low and is exposed behind the rear axle. High-centering on a rock shelf can contact the tank directly \u2014 a 3/16\" steel skid prevents that from being a trip-ending event.","steps":["Lift and support vehicle on jack stands; provide full underbody access","Remove OEM factory skid plates on Trail/TRD Pro trims \u2014 typically 4\u20136 bolts per piece","Clean mounting surfaces; inspect for corrosion at frame mounting points","Test-fit each skid section dry to confirm fitment before installing hardware","Install front crossmember/oil pan skid first; use existing OEM holes where possible","Install transmission and transfer case skid \u2014 use a floor jack as an assist for the heavy center piece","Install fuel tank skid, ensuring clearance around fuel fill neck and tank straps","Apply anti-seize to all hardware","Torque all fasteners to spec; verify clearance between skids and driveshafts by rotating shafts by hand","Lower vehicle and roll forward slowly to confirm no binding or noise from skid contact"],"pros":["ASFIR full kit covers every major underbody component in one purchase","3/16\" steel handles direct rock impacts that destroy the factory aluminum plates","Slick surfaces on steel skids allow the truck to slide over rocks rather than snag","CBI kit includes additional frame reinforcement brackets not found in factory skid mounting","Trail and TRD Pro factory plate removal is clean \u2014 aftermarket units use the same bolt holes on most designs"],"cons":["Full steel kit adds 70\u2013110 lbs of underbody weight","5th gen IFS architecture requires IFS-specific mounting design \u2014 do not use a generic skid or one not spec'd for the 5th gen","Slick Rock Fabrications kit uses lighter gauge material \u2014 adequate for mild use, may deform on a direct rock hit at speed","Some kits reduce ground clearance by 1/4\"\u20133/8\" depending on design \u2014 measure before ordering for rock-crawling use","Fuel tank skid must clear tank straps and fill neck \u2014 installation requires care to avoid pinching or stressing the tank mounting hardware"]},{"id":"4runner-sliders","title":"Rock Sliders and Frame Rails for the 5th Gen 4Runner","vehicle":"4runner","category":"armor","type":"part","difficulty":3,"time_hours":{"low":2,"high":4},"cost_usd":{"low":549,"high":899},"tools_required":["floor jack","jack stands","socket set","torque wrench","drill and bits","pry bar"],"skills_required":["basic wrenching","underbody work"],"build_tiers":["mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler"],"year_compatibility":["2010-2024"],"tags":["armor","sliders","frame-rails","rocker-protection","rock-crawling"],"parts":[{"name":"CBI Offroad 4Runner Rock Sliders (5th Gen 2010-2024)","vendor":"CBI Offroad","price_estimate_usd":749},{"name":"Shrockworks 4Runner Rock Sliders (5th Gen)","vendor":"Shrockworks","price_estimate_usd":699},{"name":"Metal Tech 4x4 4Runner Frame Sliders (5th Gen)","vendor":"Metal Tech 4x4","price_estimate_usd":649}],"citations":[{"title":"CBI Offroad 4Runner Slider Product Page","url":"https://www.cbioffroad.com"},{"title":"Shrockworks 4Runner Products","url":"https://www.shrockworks.com"}],"alternatives":["4runner-skid-plates","4runner-front-bumper"],"summary":"The 5th gen 4Runner has a pronounced lower body step and exposed pinch welds below the door sills \u2014 frame-mounted sliders protect both and give you a reliable Hi-Lift jack point that the factory body cannot provide.","description":"The 4Runner's lower body structure is more exposed than the Tacoma's. The factory step molding below the doors is plastic-capped stamped steel, and the pinch weld that runs along the rocker panel is an accessible contact point on any technical trail. Rock sliders mount to the frame and extend beyond the rocker panel, intercepting rock hits before they reach the body. On the 5th gen, most slider designs also cover the forward section of the lower rocker, which is a common first-contact point when the truck pivots over a diagonal obstacle.\n\nN-Fab nerf bars are sold in Toyota fitments and are a popular accessory for the 4Runner \u2014 but they are body-mounted and designed as cab entry steps, not trail protection. They will bend or deform under the lateral load of a rock hit or hi-lift use. If you are buying sliders for off-road protection, ensure the product specifies frame mounting with steel gusset plates at the attachment points. CBI, Shrockworks, and Metal Tech all use this construction. Shrockworks in particular has a strong reputation in the 4Runner community for weld quality and fitment precision.\n\nThe 4Runner's wider body and higher center of gravity mean side-hill situations happen on moderate trails, not just aggressive rock crawling. Sliders pay off earlier in the 4Runner's use profile than on a Tacoma. For overland builds that regularly traverse off-camber terrain or narrow shelf roads, sliders are baseline protection alongside skid plates.","steps":["Lift and support vehicle on jack stands; access the underside","Inspect frame rails for rust or damage at the planned mounting points","Test-fit slider assembly dry; confirm clearance with tires at full lock","Mark and center-punch drill locations per manufacturer template (most designs use existing body mount bolt locations plus new holes)","Drill any required new frame holes with appropriate bit; deburr edges","Apply anti-seize to all hardware","Install slider with all hardware finger-tight; align level and flush with outer rocker panel edge","Torque all fasteners to spec \u2014 typically 50\u201375 ft-lb for 1/2\" grade 8 hardware","Verify slider does not contact exhaust heat shields or brake lines at full suspension droop","Touch up raw steel edges with primer or satin black rattle can"],"pros":["Frame-mounted sliders protect pinch welds and rocker panels from the lateral rock hits that dent or crease 4Runner body panels","Hi-Lift jack points \u2014 the slider tube is the correct location to jack the 4Runner in the field","Shrockworks sliders are particularly well-regarded for weld quality and precise 4Runner fitment","CBI sliders include an integrated step tube \u2014 makes cab entry easier on a lifted 4Runner","Bolt-on install with no welding required"],"cons":["N-Fab nerf bars are not sliders \u2014 body-mounted steps cannot take a lateral load and should not be used as jack points","Adds 60\u201390 lbs to the build \u2014 consider combined weight with bumpers and skid plates","Some slider designs reduce ground clearance slightly below the rocker \u2014 verify before ordering for rock-crawling use","4Runner lower body steps (factory plastic) will require removal on some slider designs","Full-length slider options are limited for the 4Runner compared to the Tacoma market"]},{"id":"4runner-front-pads-rotors","title":"Front Brake Pads and Rotors","vehicle":"4runner","category":"brakes","type":"task","difficulty":2,"time_hours":{"low":1.5,"high":3},"cost_usd":{"low":120,"high":350},"tools_required":["19mm socket","14mm socket","torque wrench","C-clamp or caliper compressor","wire brush","brake cleaner","jack stands","lug wrench"],"skills_required":["basic wrenching","wheel removal"],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2003-2009","2010-2024"],"tags":["brakes","pads","rotors","safety-critical","maintenance"],"parts":[{"name":"Toyota OEM front brake pads (2010-2019)","part_number":"04465-35290","vendor":"Toyota","price_estimate_usd":75},{"name":"Toyota OEM front brake pads (2020-2024)","part_number":"04465-35330","vendor":"Toyota","price_estimate_usd":90},{"name":"Toyota OEM front rotors (pair)","part_number":"43512-60240","vendor":"Toyota","price_estimate_usd":220},{"name":"Akebono ProACT ceramic pads (low dust)","part_number":"ACT1303","vendor":"Akebono","price_estimate_usd":65},{"name":"Powerstop drilled & slotted rotors (alternative)","vendor":"Powerstop","price_estimate_usd":180}],"citations":[{"title":"5th Gen 4Runner Front Brake Replacement Walkthrough","url":"https://trail4runner.com/2018/03/26/replacing-brake-pads-rotors-4runner/"},{"title":"4Runner Brake Parts List and Torque Specs","url":"https://www.4runners.com/threads/brake-replacement-parts-list-5th-gen.34536/"}],"alternatives":["4runner-rear-brakes"],"summary":"Front pads on the 4Runner run 40,000\u201360,000 miles in mixed driving; the job takes under 2 hours, costs $120 for pads-only or $350 with new OEM rotors, and the caliper bracket bolts torque to 91 ft-lb.","description":"The 4Runner's front brake setup is conventional sliding-caliper, single-piston-per-side \u2014 the same architecture Toyota has used on body-on-frame trucks for decades. That's a good thing: parts are common, the procedure hasn't changed across the 4th gen (2003\u20132009) and 5th gen (2010\u20132024), and you can usually do the job with hand tools you already own.\n\nPads first, rotors when needed. Toyota's spec is to replace rotors when they fall below 27mm (factory thickness is 28mm on most years). In practice, you'll get one set of pads on a healthy rotor before warpage or scoring forces a replacement. If you off-road and dunk the rotors in water \u2014 creek crossings, mud \u2014 they may warp at 30,000 miles even when pad life is still good. That's not a defect, it's heat-cycling.\n\nPad choice matters for what you'll feel pedal-side. OEM Toyota pads (Advics or Akebono OE) are the quietest and produce moderate dust. Akebono ProACT ceramics produce less dust than OE and feel nearly identical. Avoid the bargain semi-metallic pads at chain auto parts stores \u2014 they bite harder cold but they grind your rotors faster and squeal in stop-and-go. For loaded overlanding rigs running 33s, step up to EBC Yellowstuff or Powerstop Z36 \u2014 they tolerate sustained heat from steep descents better than OE.\n\nRotors: stick with Toyota OEM or Centric Premium. Drilled-and-slotted from Powerstop or R1 Concepts look aggressive but offer zero performance benefit for a 4,800-lb truck on the street \u2014 the drill holes become stress risers and the slots wear pads faster. If you want better fade resistance, the upgrade path is bigger brakes (Powerbrake X-Line for 6th gen Tacoma calipers swap) not \"performance\" rotors on stock calipers.\n\nThe two caliper bracket bolts (19mm, 91 ft-lb) and the two caliper slider bolts (14mm, 30 ft-lb) are the critical torques. Get either wrong and the caliper either binds (rotor drag, premature pad wear, hot wheels) or works loose (vibration, eventual failure). Always use a torque wrench on brakes \u2014 they're life-safety hardware.","steps":["Loosen front lug nuts while wheels are on the ground. Lift and support the front of the truck on jack stands at the frame.","Remove front wheels. Inspect old rotor and pad thickness.","Open the brake fluid reservoir cap (relieves pressure when compressing pistons). Cover the fender with a rag.","Remove the two caliper slider bolts (14mm) on the rear of the caliper. Slide the caliper off the bracket. Hang it from a coat hanger or bungee \u2014 never let it dangle by the hose.","Pull old pads and shims out of the bracket. Note orientation.","Compress the piston back into the caliper with a C-clamp using an old pad as a buffer. Move slowly to avoid forcing fluid backward into ABS.","Remove the two caliper bracket bolts (19mm) from the steering knuckle. Lift bracket off.","Pull the old rotor. Wire-brush the hub face clean to the bare metal \u2014 even small rust ridges cause runout.","Install new rotor (lightly oil the hub face to prevent rust seizure \u2014 keep oil off the friction surface). Reinstall the bracket. Torque the bracket bolts to 91 ft-lb.","Install new pads with new anti-rattle shims/clips. Apply a thin film of brake grease to the back of the pad and to the slider pins.","Slide the caliper over the pads onto the bracket. Reinstall slider bolts and torque to 30 ft-lb.","Reinstall wheels, torque lugs to 83 ft-lb (5th gen) or 76 ft-lb (4th gen).","Pump the brake pedal 5\u20136 times until firm before driving. Top off fluid. Bed in pads with 8\u201310 moderate stops from 35 mph."],"pros":["Standard sliding caliper \u2014 no special tools, common torque sizes","Same procedure 2003\u20132024 with only pad/rotor part number changes","OEM Toyota parts under $300 for full job; $120 pads only","50K+ pad life typical for daily-driven 4Runners"],"cons":["Rotors warp from creek crossings before they wear out on heavy trail rigs","Cheap semi-metallic pads will grind rotors faster than they save you money","The 2020+ pad part number changed \u2014 verify by VIN before ordering","No factory brake upgrade path; bigger brakes mean swapping calipers from Tundra or aftermarket"]},{"id":"4runner-rear-brakes-4runner","title":"Rear Brake Pads and Rotors","vehicle":"4runner","category":"brakes","type":"task","difficulty":2,"time_hours":{"low":1,"high":2},"cost_usd":{"low":90,"high":280},"tools_required":["17mm socket","14mm socket","c-clamp or brake caliper tool","torque wrench","wire brush","brake cleaner"],"skills_required":["basic wrenching"],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","mild_trail","moderate_trail","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2010-2024"],"tags":["safety-critical","wear-item"],"parts":[{"name":"OEM Toyota Rear Brake Pad Set","part_number":"04466-60140","vendor":"Toyota","price_estimate_usd":65},{"name":"Akebono Pro-ACT Rear Pads (ceramic)","part_number":"ACT995","vendor":"Akebono","price_estimate_usd":45},{"name":"OEM rear rotor (pair)","vendor":"Toyota / Centric","price_estimate_usd":140}],"citations":[{"title":"4Runner Brake Pad and Rotor Installation guide","url":"https://trail4runner.com/2018/03/26/replacing-brake-pads-rotors-4runner/"},{"title":"How to Replace Rear Brake Pads & Rotors on a 2018 Toyota 4Runner","url":"https://howtoo.com/guide/orion/how-to-replace-rear-brake-pads-rotors-on-a-2018-toyota-4runner-diy-guide"},{"title":"Brake Replacement Parts List 5th Gen","url":"https://www.4runners.com/threads/brake-replacement-parts-list-5th-gen.34536/"}],"alternatives":["4runner-front-pads-rotors-4runner","4runner-brake-fluid-flush-4runner"],"summary":"Rear brake pads and rotors on a 5th gen 4Runner are a one-hour-per-side job; OEM pads (04466-60140) are the right choice for daily and overland duty because they handle the truck's weight without dust storms.","description":"The 5th gen 4Runner uses a single-piston floating caliper at each rear corner, sliding on two greased pins. Pads typically last 50,000\u201380,000 miles depending on how much towing and trail braking the truck sees. Rotors last for two pad sets if you bed in cleanly. Slotted or drilled rotors offer no real benefit on a 4Runner \u2014 Toyota's solid rear rotors run cool enough, and slotting just adds noise. Centric Premium or OEM-replacement smooth rotors are the right call.\n\nPad choice matters more than rotor choice. Akebono Pro-ACT ($45) is a low-dust ceramic option and is what comes on many Toyota and Lexus models from the factory. Hawk LTS ($60) bites harder when towing or descending grades but throws more dust. Avoid semi-metallic budget pads \u2014 they chew rotors and squeal cold. For a truck doing trail recovery, overland trips, or any towing, ceramic OEM-spec pads are the right balance.\n\nCaliper bracket bolts torque to 90 Nm (66 ft-lbs). Slide pin bolts torque to 34 Nm (25 ft-lbs). The slide pins must be clean and re-greased with high-temp silicone caliper grease \u2014 a dry pin is the most common cause of uneven pad wear and one-sided heat. The piston pushes straight back without rotation on this caliper, so a standard C-clamp works to retract it before pad install. Don't force it \u2014 open the bleeder on the caliper before retracting if the master cylinder is full, otherwise you push old fluid backward through the ABS unit.\n\nBed-in matters: after install, do 8 to 10 medium stops from 40 mph down to 10 mph without coming to a full stop, then drive five minutes to cool. This transfers an even layer of pad material to the rotor and is what separates a quiet new brake job from a job that pulses and squeals.","steps":["Loosen rear lug nuts, jack up rear, support on stands, remove wheels.","Remove the two caliper slide pin bolts (14mm) on the back of the caliper.","Swing the caliper up off the bracket and hang it from a coil with a wire \u2014 do not let it dangle by the hose.","Pull old pads out of the bracket; note the shim and clip orientation.","Remove the two caliper bracket bolts (17mm) to free the rotor.","Wire-brush the hub face, then slide the new rotor on.","Torque the bracket bolts to 66 ft-lbs.","Retract the caliper piston with a C-clamp, with the bleeder cracked if fluid is full.","Insert new pads with anti-rattle clips greased on the contact ears only.","Re-grease slide pins, reinstall caliper, torque slide bolts to 25 ft-lbs.","Pump the brake pedal to seat pads before driving.","Bed in with 8\u201310 medium stops from 40 to 10 mph."],"pros":["One-hour-per-side DIY with hand tools","OEM-equivalent pads keep the truck quiet and clean","Inexpensive vs. shop labor"],"cons":["Requires correct torque on slide pins to avoid uneven wear","Cheap pads squeal and chew rotors \u2014 buy decent ones","Pumping fluid backward through ABS can damage the module if you skip the bleeder step"]},{"id":"4runner-cv-axle-4runner","title":"Front CV Axle Replacement","vehicle":"4runner","category":"drivetrain","type":"task","difficulty":3,"time_hours":{"low":2,"high":4},"cost_usd":{"low":150,"high":450},"tools_required":["floor jack","jack stands","35mm socket","19mm socket","breaker bar","torque wrench","pry bar","hammer","anti-seize","wire brush"],"skills_required":["basic wrenching","front-end disassembly"],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2010-2024"],"tags":["drivetrain","axles"],"parts":[{"name":"Toyota OEM Front CV Axle (L/R) \u2014 5th Gen","part_number":"43430-60083","vendor":"Toyota","price_estimate_usd":420},{"name":"Toyota Updated Axle Nut","part_number":"90177-A0023","vendor":"Toyota","price_estimate_usd":8},{"name":"GSP Premium Loaded CV Axle","vendor":"GSP","price_estimate_usd":145},{"name":"Cardone Select New CV Axle","vendor":"Cardone","price_estimate_usd":160}],"citations":[{"title":"How To Replace Front CV Axle \u2014 4Runners.com","url":"https://www.4runners.com/threads/how-to-replace-front-cv-axle.2527/"},{"title":"Toyota 4Runner Front End Torque Specs (2020)","url":"https://toyota-specs.com/cars/2020/4runner4.0/torque-specs_front.php"},{"title":"OEM Toyota CV Axle 43430-60083","url":"https://parts.toyotaofnashua.com/oem-parts/toyota-2023-2024-toyota-4runner-cv-axle-left-right-front-4343060083"},{"title":"5th Gen 4Runner CV Boot Replacement Guide","url":"https://trail4runner.com/2023/08/30/cv-boot-replacement-guide-5th-gen-4runner/"}],"alternatives":["4runner-tie-rods-4runner","4runner-lower-ball-joint-4runner"],"summary":"Replace a 5th-gen 4Runner front CV axle with a Toyota OEM unit (PN 43430-60083) in two to four hours; torque the axle nut to 174 ft-lbs and use the updated nut PN 90177-A0023 with new OEM axles.","description":"Front CV axles on the 5th gen 4Runner (2010-onward) and the related GX460 typically fail from torn boots first. The boot rips on rocks or against a poorly clearanced UCA after a lift, grease flings out, the joint runs dry, and within a few thousand miles you have a clicking sound on tight turns under throttle. Once you hear the click, the joint is done \u2014 re-booting at that stage is a temporary fix. Replace the axle assembly.\n\nToyota OEM is the honest answer for stock or moderately lifted 4Runners that see daily driving. The OEM axle (43430-60083 fits both sides on 2011-2024 4Runners and FJ Cruisers) is rebuildable, the boots last twice as long as most aftermarket boots, and the joint stays quiet at full droop with lift. Expect about $400-450 for the OEM unit. Note that current production OEM axles require the updated axle nut (90177-A0023) \u2014 buy two if you're doing both sides.\n\nAftermarket options like GSP Premium Loaded or Cardone Select run $140-170 and are acceptable for stock-height 4Runners. They will not hold up as well on a lifted truck running 33s or 34s and dropping into the throttle in 4-low. The cheapest econo-brand axles ($60-90) bind at lift-height droop angles and tear boots within a year \u2014 skip them.\n\nIf you are lifted more than 2.5 inches up front, you are running the CV at angles it was never designed to operate at. The two things that prolong axle life on a lifted 4Runner are: (1) limiting down-travel with proper bumpstops so the CV never goes past its safe angle, and (2) upgrading to aftermarket upper control arms that re-locate the upper ball joint to keep the spindle more vertical. Without those changes, even an OEM CV axle is on a clock.\n\nCommon mistake: forgetting to back off the axle nut before lifting the truck. The nut requires 174 ft-lbs of torque to remove and the wheel needs to be on the ground (or a helper holding the brake) so the hub doesn't spin.","steps":["Loosen the front lug nuts, then loosen the axle nut while the wheel is still on the ground \u2014 staking-clip side must be unpeened first using a punch.","Jack the front, set jack stands under the frame rails, and remove the wheel.","Pull the brake caliper off and hang it from the coil spring with mechanic's wire. Do not unhook the brake line.","Remove the rotor.","Disconnect the outer tie rod from the steering knuckle (19mm castle nut, cotter pin first).","Loosen and remove the two lower control arm bolts at the spindle (19mm). Do not disturb the LCA-to-frame bolts \u2014 those affect alignment.","Pull the axle nut off completely. Swing the knuckle outward and pull the CV stub out of the hub. If it's stuck, give it a few persuasive hits with a brass drift on the threaded end.","Pry the inner CV joint out of the front differential with a pry bar against the inner cup. Catch the small amount of diff fluid that leaks.","Wire-brush both the hub splines and the differential output splines. Apply anti-seize to the hub splines.","Slide the new axle's inner joint into the differential \u2014 it should click into the C-clip with firm hand pressure. If it won't seat, you'll need to tap it home gently with a soft mallet.","Reconnect the outer stub through the hub, reinstall the LCA bolts and torque to 118 ft-lbs, reconnect the tie rod (67 ft-lbs castle nut, new cotter pin).","Install the NEW axle nut (PN 90177-A0023 if running new OEM axle). Torque to 174 ft-lbs with the wheel on the ground. Stake the nut collar into the groove on the axle.","Reinstall rotor, caliper, and wheel. Torque lug nuts to 83 ft-lbs.","Check front diff fluid level \u2014 top off if any was lost during the swap."],"pros":["Stops the clicking and prevents stranding when the joint finally lets go","OEM-quality axles last 100,000+ miles on a stock-height truck","Clean access compared to many other vehicles \u2014 no ABS sensor or vacuum disconnect to fight"],"cons":["Axle nut requires impact tools or a long breaker bar to break free","Old axle inner joint can be stubborn out of the differential \u2014 wear safety glasses","C-clip on the inner stub must seat fully or the axle will work itself loose under throttle"]},{"id":"4runner-locker-rear","title":"Rear Locker for the 5th Gen 4Runner \u2014 ARB, Eaton, and TRD","vehicle":"4runner","category":"drivetrain","type":"part","difficulty":4,"time_hours":{"low":3,"high":6},"cost_usd":{"low":649,"high":999},"tools_required":["floor jack","jack stands","socket set","torque wrench","dial indicator","gear oil","RTV sealant","bearing press or access to press"],"skills_required":["differential service","gear setup basics","bearing press operation"],"build_tiers":["moderate_trail","rock_crawler"],"year_compatibility":["2010-2024"],"tags":["locker","rear-locker","differential","traction","ARB","TRD","rock-crawling"],"parts":[{"name":"ARB Air Locker for Toyota 8.9\" Rear Differential (5th Gen 4Runner)","vendor":"ARB 4x4 Accessories","price_estimate_usd":849},{"name":"Eaton ELocker for Toyota 8.9\" (5th Gen 4Runner)","vendor":"Eaton","price_estimate_usd":699},{"name":"TRD Mechanical Locker (dealer-installed factory option, 4Runner Trail/TRD Pro)","vendor":"Toyota Racing Development","price_estimate_usd":650}],"citations":[{"title":"ARB Air Locker Toyota 8.9\" Product Page","url":"https://www.arbusa.com"},{"title":"Eaton ELocker Toyota Product Page","url":"https://www.eaton.com/us/en-us/catalog/vehicle/eaton-elocker.html"}],"alternatives":["4runner-mid-lift","4runner-expedition-lift"],"summary":"The 5th gen 4Runner uses a Toyota 8.9\" ring gear rear differential \u2014 the ARB Air Locker and Eaton ELocker are the two quality aftermarket options, and the TRD mechanical locker is the factory bolt-on solution available on Trail and TRD Pro trims.","description":"The 4Runner Trail Edition and TRD Pro models come from the factory with a TRD mechanical locker in the rear differential \u2014 it's a mechanical cam-and-pawl design that engages both rear wheels when actuated via a dash switch. If your 4Runner is a Trail or TRD Pro trim, you already have a rear locker. SR5 and Limited 4Runners do not have a factory locker and require aftermarket installation.\n\nThe ARB Air Locker for the Toyota 8.9\" rear axle is the highest-performance aftermarket option. When disengaged, it behaves exactly like a factory open differential \u2014 no on-road driving compromises. When engaged via air pressure, both rear wheels lock together for full traction in low-grip situations. The ARB locker requires an air compressor (dedicated ARB compressor, $400\u2013$700, or a dual-use on-board air system) and air line routing to the differential. The total system cost including the compressor is $1,250\u2013$1,550.\n\nThe Eaton ELocker eliminates the air requirement. It uses an electromagnetic coil to lock the carrier \u2014 flip the in-cab switch and both rear wheels lock. The engagement is clean and on-road behavior when disengaged is identical to an open differential. For builds where on-board air is not already planned (no air bumper, no air lockers front and rear), the Eaton ELocker is the most cost-effective and installation-friendly option.\n\nThe 4Runner 8.9\" rear differential uses a standard gear setup that most shops familiar with Toyota axles can service. Backlash spec is 0.005\"\u20130.008\" and carrier bearing preload follows Toyota's standard procedure. This is the same differential used in Tundra and Land Cruiser 200 Series applications, so parts availability and shop familiarity are good.","steps":["Verify rear axle ring gear size and carrier configuration \u2014 confirm 8.9\" application and 4WD vs 2WD carrier type","Drain rear differential gear oil","Remove rear axle shafts (backing plate nuts, pull shafts straight out)","Remove differential cover; clean all gasket surfaces","Remove carrier bearing caps \u2014 mark left/right orientation clearly before removal","Remove carrier assembly; note original shim positions at each bearing cup","Press out OEM side gears, spider gears, and cross-shaft","Install locker internals per manufacturer installation guide \u2014 ARB and Eaton both include detailed instructions","For ARB: install air fitting in differential cover; route air line through axle housing to bulkhead fitting","Reinstall carrier with original shims; set backlash to 0.005\"\u20130.008\" spec","Verify bearing preload with dial indicator per Toyota spec","Install differential cover with new gasket or RTV; torque bolts in star pattern","Reinstall axle shafts; torque backing plate nuts to spec","Refill with Toyota-spec GL-5 75W-90 gear oil","For ARB: route and connect air line to compressor; test engagement before trail use","For Eaton: route wiring harness to cab switch; test engagement"],"pros":["ARB Air Locker delivers full on-demand locking with zero on-road compromise when disengaged","Eaton ELocker requires no air system \u2014 cleaner install for builds without on-board air","TRD factory locker on Trail/TRD Pro trim is the simplest solution if you can start with the right trim","Toyota 8.9\" is a robust axle that handles locker loads well for the 4Runner's use profile","Rear locker provides traction that lift and tires cannot deliver \u2014 essential for serious rocky terrain"],"cons":["ARB Air Locker total system cost including compressor is $1,250\u2013$1,550","Differential carrier disassembly requires a bearing press and familiarity with gear setup \u2014 professional install recommended for first-time locker installers","Incorrect backlash setting causes premature ring-and-pinion wear","TRD dealer locker pricing varies by region \u2014 confirm current dealer pricing before planning","Eaton ELocker wiring harness routing through the firewall adds install time compared to the ARB air line approach on trucks already running on-board air"]},{"id":"4runner-alternator-replacement","title":"Alternator Replacement","vehicle":"4runner","category":"electrical","subcategory":"charging","type":"task","difficulty":3,"time_hours":{"low":2,"high":3},"cost_usd":{"low":220,"high":750},"tools_required":["floor jack","jack stands","10mm socket","12mm socket","14mm socket","serpentine belt tool","torque wrench"],"skills_required":["serpentine belt routing","basic electrical"],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2003-2009","2010-2024"],"tags":["1GR-FE","charging","winch","dual-battery"],"parts":[{"name":"OEM Toyota alternator 130A (5th gen 2010-2021)","part_number":"27060-31190","vendor":"Toyota","price_estimate_usd":540},{"name":"Denso 130A reman alternator (4.0L 1GR-FE)","part_number":"104210-2864","vendor":"Denso","price_estimate_usd":320},{"name":"DC Power 270A XP high-output alternator (5th gen)","part_number":"11517-270XP","vendor":"DC Power Inc.","price_estimate_usd":740},{"name":"Aftermarket 130A direct-fit (BBB / Quality-Built / Remy)","vendor":"Various","price_estimate_usd":220}],"citations":[{"title":"Toyota OEM 27060-31190 Alternator \u2014 American Toyota Parts","url":"https://parts.americantoyota.com/oem-parts/toyota-alternator-2706031190"},{"title":"Rareelectrical 130A Alternator 1GR-FE Specs","url":"https://www.rareelectrical.com/i-26933570-130a-alternator-compatible-with-2010-2021-toyota-4runner-v6-4-0l-1gr-fe-dohc-sr5-limited-trail-trd.html"},{"title":"DC Power 270A XP High-Output Alternator \u2014 5th Gen 4Runner","url":"https://www.dcpowerinc.com/products/11517-270-xp-toyota-4runner-2011-4"}],"alternatives":["4runner-engine-air-filter","tacoma-alternator"],"summary":"A 4Runner alternator that's failing usually shows a dim battery light or AC ripple on the bus before it quits; OEM replacement is a Denso 130A unit, and if you're running a winch or fridge, the DC Power 270A XP is the high-output upgrade that fits the same bracket.","description":"The 1GR-FE 4.0L V6 in 4th gen (2003\u20132009) and 5th gen (2010\u20132024) 4Runners uses a 130-amp Denso alternator with an SD7 7-groove decoupler pulley. OEM part number is 27060-31190. The decoupler pulley is important \u2014 it lets the alternator overrun briefly during shift changes, which smooths belt behavior and reduces shock load on the bearings. Cheap aftermarket alternators sometimes ship with a solid pulley; that's a no for 1GR-FE. Get the decoupler version.\n\nSymptoms of a failing alternator: battery light flickering at idle, dim headlights that brighten with rev, a whining noise that scales with engine RPM, accessories cycling on and off, or a multimeter reading at the battery below 13.8V with the engine running (a healthy charge is 13.8\u201314.4V). Don't replace based on the dash light alone \u2014 a load-test at a parts store (free at AutoZone, O'Reilly, Advance) confirms it before you spend the money. The other failure that looks like an alternator is a corroded ground strap from engine to chassis; check both before condemning the alternator.\n\nAccess on the 1GR-FE is from above. Unlike some V6s, you don't have to drop the front skid or pull the radiator fan. The alternator sits high on the passenger side, behind the serpentine pulley path. Belt comes off with a long ratcheting wrench on the tensioner pulley. Hardware is two main bolts plus the back-of-alternator B+ terminal (10mm nut) and the field plug. Total tool list is short. The fight, when it happens, is wiggling the alternator out past the AC line and coolant hose \u2014 patience and a hand at the right angle gets it out.\n\nIf you're a stock daily driver, the 130A OEM (or reman Denso) is right-sized. If you've added a winch, a fridge, off-road lights, or a dual-battery system, look at high-output. The DC Power Inc. 270A XP is a popular direct-fit upgrade for the 5th gen \u2014 same bracket, same harness, more than double the output at idle. Note that you may need a heavier B+ cable to handle the added current; the stock 6-gauge is sized for 130A.","steps":["Disconnect the negative battery terminal.","Loosen the serpentine belt with a ratcheting wrench on the tensioner pulley. Slip the belt off the alternator pulley.","Remove the rubber boot from the alternator B+ terminal. Loosen the 10mm nut and pull the cable off.","Unplug the field connector (squeeze tab and pull).","Remove the upper and lower alternator bolts (typically 14mm).","Wiggle the alternator out of its bracket. AC line and coolant hose are in the way \u2014 work slowly.","Compare old to new for pulley type, mounting points, and connector orientation.","Install new alternator in reverse order. Torque mounting bolts to 30 ft-lbs (lower) and 18 ft-lbs (upper) per shop manual. Torque B+ nut to 10 ft-lbs.","Route belt per under-hood diagram. Tensioner snaps back into place.","Reconnect negative battery, start engine, verify 13.8\u201314.4V at the battery."],"pros":["OEM Denso reman is reliable and uses the right decoupler pulley","Bolt-in upgrade to 270A for winch and accessories","Tool list is short","Access on 1GR-FE is from above \u2014 no skid pan removal"],"cons":["Wrong pulley type from cheap aftermarket can shred belts","High-output may need heavier B+ cable","Dash light alone is not enough \u2014 load-test first","AC line and coolant hose make removal tight"]},{"id":"4runner-atf-flush","title":"ATF Flush \u2014 A750F 5-Speed Automatic","vehicle":"4runner","category":"maintenance","subcategory":"drivetrain","type":"task","difficulty":3,"time_hours":{"low":1.5,"high":3},"cost_usd":{"low":90,"high":180},"tools_required":["24mm socket (refill plug)","14mm socket (drain plug)","5mm hex bit (check plug)","10mm socket (pan bolts)","torque wrench (in-lb and ft-lb)","fluid transfer pump","infrared thermometer or OBD-II scanner","drain pan"],"skills_required":["basic wrenching","ATF temperature check"],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","mild_trail","moderate_trail","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2003-2009","2010-2024"],"tags":["maintenance","transmission","drivetrain","atf"],"parts":[{"name":"Toyota Genuine ATF WS (1 qt)","part_number":"00289-ATFWS","vendor":"Toyota","price_estimate_usd":12},{"name":"Aftermarket Toyota WS ATF (Idemitsu, Aisin)","vendor":"Idemitsu / Aisin","price_estimate_usd":9},{"name":"Transmission pan gasket (if dropping pan)","vendor":"Toyota / OEM","price_estimate_usd":35},{"name":"Transmission filter (if servicing internally)","vendor":"Toyota / Aisin","price_estimate_usd":75}],"citations":[{"title":"Trail4Runner \u2014 Drain and Fill 5th Gen","url":"https://trail4runner.com/2023/05/07/transmission-fluid-change-5th-gen-4runner/"},{"title":"BobIsTheOilGuy \u2014 Toyota A750E/F DIY Flush Procedure","url":"https://bobistheoilguy.com/forums/threads/toyota-a750e-f-do-it-yourself-transmission-flush.283617/"},{"title":"TheTrackAhead \u2014 Transmission Fluid Flush 4th Gen 4Runner","url":"https://thetrackahead.com/projects/4th-gen-toyota-4runner/transmission-fluid-flush-and-fill-4th-gen-toyota-4runner/"}],"alternatives":[],"summary":"A drain-and-fill on the A750F pulls about 4 quarts of Toyota WS ATF; a full system flush takes 11\u201312 quarts because the torque converter holds half the fluid you can't drain in one pass.","description":"The 4th-gen (2003\u20132009) and 5th-gen (2010\u20132024) 4Runner V6 share the A750F 5-speed automatic, an Aisin-built sealed transmission with no dipstick. Toyota markets it as \"lifetime\" fluid, which is marketing \u2014 the fluid darkens and shears down between 60k and 90k miles, and 4Runners that see trailer towing, sustained Phoenix heat, or low-range crawling on washboard show ATF degradation well before that. A 60k-mile drain-and-fill is realistic preventive maintenance. A full flush (3 drain-and-fills with drives in between) at 90k\u2013100k miles is appropriate if the fluid has never been changed.\n\nFluid choice is non-negotiable: Toyota Genuine ATF WS (P/N 00289-ATFWS) or a verified equivalent (Idemitsu TLS-LV, Aisin AFW+). Toyota's own service manual warns that wrong-spec fluid will harm shift quality and damage the valve body. The cheaper \"universal\" Dexron/Mercon blends are not interchangeable here \u2014 they will shift harshly and shorten the transmission's life. Budget 12 quarts of WS to do a full flush.\n\nA drain-and-fill removes about 4 quarts, leaving roughly 7\u20138 quarts of old fluid in the torque converter, valve body, and cooler lines. To dilute old fluid effectively, do three drain-and-fills with 100-mile drives in between. After the third, you've cycled the new fluid through every chamber and the system reads about 90% fresh. Skipping the multi-pass cycle leaves you with 60\u201370% fresh fluid \u2014 better than nothing, but not a true service.\n\nThe level-check procedure is the trickiest part. There is no dipstick. Fluid level is verified by draining the overflow tube while the transmission is at exactly 104\u2013113\u00b0F (40\u201345\u00b0C). Too cold and you'll overfill; too hot and you'll underfill. Use an OBD-II scanner that reads transmission temperature (a basic Bluetooth ELM327 + Torque app works) or an infrared thermometer aimed at the pan. Loosen the 5mm hex check plug at temperature and let excess drip until flow reduces to a slow dribble, then torque to 15 ft-lbs.","steps":["Drive the vehicle 15 minutes to warm the transmission to operating temperature, then park level.","Remove the 24mm refill plug FIRST on top of the transmission (under a heat shield on some trims). If you can't get it loose, stop \u2014 you can't refill what you drain.","Position a drain pan under the transmission. Remove the 14mm drain plug and let fluid drain fully (10 minutes).","Measure the drained fluid \u2014 you'll typically capture 3.7\u20134.0 quarts. Inspect for burnt smell, metal flakes, or discoloration.","Reinstall and torque the drain plug to 21 ft-lbs.","Pump in the same quantity of fresh Toyota WS through the refill plug using a transfer pump.","Reinstall and torque the refill plug to 29 ft-lbs.","Start the engine. With your foot on the brake, cycle the shifter through P-R-N-D-3-2-L and back to P, pausing 3 seconds in each gear.","Drive 100 miles of mixed driving to circulate the new fluid through the torque converter and valve body.","Repeat steps 1\u20139 twice more for a true flush (three drain-and-fills total).","After the final fill: with engine idling and transmission at 104\u2013113\u00b0F, loosen the 5mm hex check plug.","Let excess fluid dribble out. When flow slows to drops, torque the check plug to 15 ft-lbs and shut off the engine."],"pros":["Catches metal debris and fluid breakdown before they damage the valve body","$100 in fluid vs. $4,000+ for a rebuilt transmission","A750F responds well to fresh WS \u2014 shift quality noticeably improves","Multi-pass drain-and-fill is safer than a machine flush on high-mileage units"],"cons":["No dipstick \u2014 level check requires temperature accuracy","Toyota WS is roughly $10/qt and you'll need 12 quarts for a full flush","Three drives between fills means this is a weekend job, not an hour","Refill plug can be seized at high mileage \u2014 break loose before draining"]},{"id":"4runner-brake-fluid-flush-4runner","title":"Brake Fluid Flush","vehicle":"4runner","category":"maintenance","type":"task","difficulty":2,"time_hours":{"low":1,"high":2},"cost_usd":{"low":25,"high":70},"tools_required":["10mm box wrench","clear vinyl tubing","catch bottle","turkey baster or fluid pump","rags"],"skills_required":["basic wrenching","two-person bleeding or vacuum pump"],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","mild_trail","moderate_trail","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2003-2009","2010-2024"],"tags":["brake-service","maintenance-interval","safety-critical"],"parts":[{"name":"Genuine Toyota DOT 3 brake fluid (1 qt)","part_number":"00475-1BF03","vendor":"Toyota","price_estimate_usd":12},{"name":"Valvoline DOT 3/4 brake fluid (32 oz)","vendor":"Valvoline","price_estimate_usd":10},{"name":"Motul DOT 5.1 brake fluid (1L)","vendor":"Motul","price_estimate_usd":22},{"name":"Mityvac vacuum bleeder kit","part_number":"MV8000","vendor":"Mityvac","price_estimate_usd":55}],"citations":[{"title":"Toyota 4Runner Brake Fluid Flush \u2014 Top4Runners","url":"https://www.top4runners.com/ja/2007/brakeflush.html"},{"title":"Bleeding Brakes on a 5th Gen 4Runner \u2014 Trail4Runner","url":"https://trail4runner.com/2021/10/21/bleeding-brakes-fluid-5th-gen-4runner/"},{"title":"Toyota 4Runner Brake Fluid Capacity Discussion","url":"https://www.toyotanation.com/threads/whats-the-brake-fluid-capacity.1185666/"}],"alternatives":[],"summary":"Flush your 4Runner's brake fluid every 30,000 miles or 3 years; you need about 1 quart of DOT 3, a 10mm wrench, and clear tubing to do it right.","description":"Brake fluid is hygroscopic \u2014 it absorbs moisture from the air. After three years, even sealed brake systems pick up enough water to drop the boiling point by 30\u00b0C or more. On a stock 4Runner that's a slow-degradation issue. On a heavy 4Runner running 33s or doing long downhill grades in 4Lo, that water turns to vapor under sustained braking and your pedal goes to the floor. This is the failure mode behind most \"my brakes just disappeared\" complaints on Tacoma World, IH8MUD, and the 4Runner forums.\n\nToyota specifies DOT 3 from the factory and the fill cap says so. DOT 4 is compatible and has a higher dry boiling point, which makes it the right choice for a rig that does long descents or runs big tires. DOT 5.1 is also glycol-based and compatible with DOT 3/4. The only fluid you must never use is DOT 5 \u2014 that's silicone-based, will not mix with DOT 3/4, and will destroy the ABS unit. The bottles look similar; read the label.\n\nCapacity for a full flush is 1 quart on a 4th gen (2003\u20132009) and 1\u20131.5 quarts on a 5th gen (2010\u20132024) because the longer brake lines hold more fluid. Buy two quarts. You'll use about 1.2 quarts for the flush and keep the second for top-offs over the next year. Always use a fresh, sealed container \u2014 opened brake fluid begins absorbing water immediately and is suspect after 12 months on a shelf.\n\nOrder of bleeding is farthest from master cylinder to nearest: right rear, left rear, right front, left front. The 5th gen's ABS/VSC system holds fluid in the modulator and won't fully flush from a bench bleed \u2014 for a complete flush, Toyota Techstream or a service tool that cycles the ABS pump is recommended, but for a routine fluid swap the standard four-corner bleed is sufficient. Watch the reservoir level constantly \u2014 if it runs dry you've sucked air into the master cylinder and the job just doubled in length.","steps":["Park on level ground, engine off, parking brake set.","Pop the hood, locate the brake fluid reservoir on the driver-side firewall.","Use a turkey baster or fluid pump to remove old fluid from the reservoir, leaving 1/4\" in the bottom.","Refill the reservoir to MAX with fresh DOT 3 or DOT 4.","Start at the right rear caliper. Place a box wrench on the bleeder valve (10mm).","Attach clear vinyl tubing to the bleeder, route the free end into a catch bottle.","Helper pumps brake pedal 3 times, holds on the third pump.","Crack the bleeder 1/4 turn \u2014 fluid and any air come out. Helper holds pedal to floor.","Close the bleeder before helper releases the pedal.","Repeat until clear new fluid runs out \u2014 about 4\u20136 cycles per corner.","Check reservoir level after every two bleeds; never let it drop below MIN.","Move to left rear, then right front, then left front. Same procedure.","Top up reservoir to MAX with fresh fluid.","Pump pedal to verify firm feel before moving the vehicle.","Test brakes at low speed before normal driving."],"pros":["Restores brake pedal feel","Prevents brake fade on long descents","Cheap insurance against a vapor-lock incident","1\u20132 hour job at home with $25 in fluid"],"cons":["DOT 5 contamination requires a full system rebuild","Letting the reservoir run dry adds an hour of master cylinder bleeding","ABS unit on 5th gen won't fully flush without a scan tool that cycles the pump","Brake fluid eats paint \u2014 wipe spills immediately"]},{"id":"4runner-cabin-filter-4runner","title":"Cabin Air Filter Replacement","vehicle":"4runner","category":"maintenance","subcategory":"cabin_filter","type":"task","difficulty":1,"time_hours":{"low":0.15,"high":0.3},"cost_usd":{"low":12,"high":35},"tools_required":["your hands"],"skills_required":["follow tabs and clips"],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2003-2009","2010-2024"],"tags":["cabin-filter","hvac","maintenance","5-minute-job"],"parts":[{"name":"Toyota OEM cabin air filter (5th gen)","part_number":"87139-YZZ20","vendor":"Toyota","price_estimate_usd":22},{"name":"Premium Guard cabin filter","part_number":"PC5667","vendor":"Premium Guard","price_estimate_usd":14},{"name":"EPAuto carbon-activated cabin filter","part_number":"CP285 (CF10285)","vendor":"EPAuto","price_estimate_usd":16}],"citations":[{"title":"PartsGeek \u2014 2010-2024 4Runner cabin air filter PC5667 fitment","url":"https://www.partsgeek.com/j5tkrv8-toyota-4runner-cabin-air-filter.html"},{"title":"Toyota Parts Deal \u2014 4Runner OEM cabin air filter","url":"https://www.toyotapartsdeal.com/oem-toyota-4runner-cabin_air_filter.html"},{"title":"4Runners.com \u2014 Cabin filter part number clarification","url":"https://www.4runners.com/threads/cabin-air-filter-part-number-clarification-please.26561/"}],"alternatives":["4runner-engine-air-filter-4runner"],"summary":"The 4Runner cabin air filter sits behind the glove box. Replacement is a five-minute job with no tools \u2014 drop the glove box, slide out the filter tray, swap the element, slide it back. Toyota recommends every 15,000\u201330,000 miles. Dusty Phoenix or dirt-road use cuts that to 10,000.","description":"Toyota put the cabin filter in one of the friendliest locations in the industry. There are no covers to pry off, no fasteners to remove, no kneeling on the floor \u2014 just the glove box itself swinging down on its two stops.\n\n**Why it matters.** The cabin filter catches everything before it hits the HVAC evaporator. A clogged filter reduces airflow, lets the evaporator collect dust and moisture (which grows mildew \u2014 that musty smell on first start), and makes the blower work harder. On 5th-gen 4Runners (2010\u20132024) the factory filter is a basic paper element; aftermarket carbon-activated versions filter odors and fumes from the truck ahead of you, which matters more than people think in dirt-road convoys.\n\n**5th gen vs. 4th gen.** Both generations use a glove-box-access filter. 5th-gen (2010\u20132024) takes Toyota part 87139-YZZ20 (or aftermarket PC5667, CP285). 4th-gen (2003\u20132009) takes a different filter \u2014 87139-YZZ08 \u2014 and the housing is slightly smaller. The aftermarket cross-references usually call this out clearly. Check the box before you install.\n\n**Direction matters.** The filter has an arrow on the frame indicating airflow direction. Install with the arrow pointing down (toward the floor on most 4Runners \u2014 confirm against the printing on the housing). Backward orientation reduces filtration and can cause the pleats to collapse over time.\n\n**Change interval.** Toyota's maintenance schedule says 30,000 miles. In real-world use that's optimistic. If you live somewhere dusty (Phoenix, the Mountain West, anywhere with monsoon dust storms), or wheel in dirt regularly, plan on 10,000\u201315,000 miles. Pull it out and look \u2014 if it's gray and packed with debris, replace it. They cost $15.","steps":["Open the glove box.","Empty the contents.","Squeeze the sides of the glove box inward to release the two side stops. The box swings all the way down to the floor.","The cabin filter housing is the rectangular plastic cover directly behind where the glove box was \u2014 about 6\" wide, 2\" tall.","Push in the two tabs on the cover and pull the cover toward you. The filter tray slides out with the cover.","Note the airflow arrow direction on the old filter. Discard the old filter.","Slide the new filter into the tray with the airflow arrow matching the original orientation (arrow down).","Slide the tray back into the housing until the tabs click.","Lift the glove box back into position, compressing the side stops so it seats fully."],"pros":["Five-minute job","No tools required","$15 part","Restores HVAC airflow and prevents evap mildew"],"cons":["4th-gen and 5th-gen filters are different \u2014 verify part number","Carbon filters cost more but catch odors stock paper does not","Arrow direction matters \u2014 backwards installation reduces filtration","Some aftermarket filters fit loosely \u2014 Toyota OEM and Premium Guard PC5667 are the tightest fits"]},{"id":"4runner-engine-air-filter-4runner","title":"Engine Air Filter Replacement","vehicle":"4runner","category":"maintenance","type":"task","difficulty":1,"time_hours":{"low":0.1,"high":0.25},"cost_usd":{"low":15,"high":50},"tools_required":["flathead screwdriver (optional)"],"skills_required":["none"],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2003-2009","2010-2024"],"tags":["maintenance","filter","intake"],"parts":[{"name":"Toyota OEM Engine Air Filter (5th gen)","part_number":"17801-38051","vendor":"Toyota","price_estimate_usd":25},{"name":"Toyota OEM Engine Air Filter (updated)","part_number":"17801-YZZ13","vendor":"Toyota","price_estimate_usd":25},{"name":"FRAM Extra Guard (alternative)","vendor":"FRAM","price_estimate_usd":18}],"citations":[{"title":"Toyota OEM 17801-38051 Air Filter","url":"https://autoparts.toyota.com/products/product/element-sub-assy-air-cleaner-filter-1780138051"},{"title":"5th Gen Engine Air Filter Discussion","url":"https://www.4runners.com/threads/oem-filters-part-numbers.21896/"}],"alternatives":["4runner-cabin-air-filter-4runner"],"summary":"The 4Runner engine air filter is a five-minute swap with no tools required on most years \u2014 pull the four clips, lift the airbox lid, drop the new filter in, close it up.","description":"Toyota recommends changing the engine air filter every 30,000 miles under normal driving conditions, and sooner if you spend significant time on dirt roads or trails. Phoenix-area owners, anyone who runs Forest Service roads regularly, or anyone who's done a Baja-style trip should plan on 15,000\u201320,000 miles instead. A clogged filter doesn't usually throw a code \u2014 it quietly costs you fuel economy and throttle response.\n\nThe 5th gen 4Runner (2010\u20132024) uses Toyota part number 17801-38051, later superseded by 17801-YZZ13. The two are interchangeable. Aftermarket options from FRAM, K&N, AEM, and Wix all fit and run $15\u2013$50. Reusable oiled cotton filters like K&N flow more air but require periodic cleaning and reoiling, and over-oiling has been linked to MAF sensor contamination. For most owners, a paper OEM-style replacement is the right call.\n\nThe filter box sits on the passenger side of the engine bay, near the firewall. Open the hood and you'll see the black plastic box with metal clips on the lid. There's no airflow direction arrow that matters \u2014 the pleats just need to face down so debris drops into the body of the airbox rather than the lid.\n\nInspect the airbox interior while you have it open. Mouse nests, leaves, and acorns are common in vehicles that sit, and any of those will get drawn through a fresh filter quickly. Vacuum or wipe out anything you find before installing the new element.","steps":["Open the hood and locate the black plastic air filter box on the passenger side, against the firewall.","Release the four metal spring clips on the airbox lid. They flip up with finger pressure or a small flathead.","Lift the lid hinged toward the engine \u2014 the intake hose acts as a hinge, so you don't need to disconnect anything.","Pull the old filter straight up and out. Note the orientation (pleats face down toward the body of the box).","Wipe or vacuum any debris from inside the airbox.","Drop the new filter in with pleats facing down. It should sit flat with no gaps around the seal.","Lower the lid and reseat the four clips. Make sure they're fully latched \u2014 a partially seated lid lets unfiltered air past.","Close the hood. Done."],"pros":["No tools, no jacks, no fluids","Five-minute job","Most-skipped maintenance item with real fuel economy and throttle response payoff"],"cons":["OEM filters cost more than paper alternatives but fit precisely","Oiled-cotton filters can contaminate MAF sensors if over-oiled"]},{"id":"4runner-front-diff-fluid-4runner","title":"Front Differential Fluid Change","vehicle":"4runner","category":"maintenance","type":"task","difficulty":1,"time_hours":{"low":0.5,"high":1},"cost_usd":{"low":25,"high":50},"tools_required":["24mm socket or hex bit","fluid pump","drain pan","torque wrench"],"skills_required":["basic wrenching"],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2003-2009","2010-2024","2025-2026"],"tags":["axles","gear-oil","service-interval","1gr-fe"],"parts":[{"name":"Toyota OEM 75W-85 GL-5 differential gear oil (1 quart)","part_number":"08885-02506","vendor":"Toyota","price_estimate_usd":18},{"name":"Toyota OEM 75W-85 GL-5 (alternate part #)","part_number":"08885-02906","vendor":"Toyota","price_estimate_usd":18},{"name":"Toyota OEM fill/drain crush gasket","part_number":"12157-10010","vendor":"Toyota","price_estimate_usd":2},{"name":"Valvoline SynPower 75W-90 GL-5 (1 quart, alternate)","part_number":"VV975","vendor":"Valvoline","price_estimate_usd":13}],"citations":[{"title":"BlauParts \u2014 Toyota 4Runner Differential Fluid Gear Oil Type Guide","url":"https://www.blauparts.com/blog/what-type-of-differential-fluid-does-my-toyota-4runner-take.html"},{"title":"Trail4R \u2014 4Runner Driveline Fluids: How and When to Change Them","url":"https://trail4runner.com/2018/12/24/4runner-driveline-fluids-how-and-when-to-change-them/"},{"title":"The Track Ahead \u2014 Front and Rear Differential Fluid Replacement: 4th Gen Toyota 4Runner","url":"https://thetrackahead.com/projects/4th-gen-toyota-4runner/front-and-rear-differential-fluid-replacement-4th-gen-toyota-4runner/"}],"alternatives":[],"summary":"The 4Runner front differential takes about 1.3 quarts of 75W-85 GL-5 gear oil and Toyota's recommended interval is 30,000 miles for severe service \u2014 the 1GR-FE platform has run reliably for two decades because owners actually do this.","description":"The 1GR-FE 4.0L V6 4Runner uses a conventional open or limited-slip front differential. It's not part-time disconnect like a Wrangler \u2014 even in 2WD the front ring and pinion are turning, just not driving. That means the fluid sees thermal cycling even when you're not in 4HI, and on a 4Runner that lives in Arizona heat or pulls a trailer, the front diff fluid breaks down faster than most owners assume.\n\nToyota's current spec is 75W-85 GL-5 gear oil. The OEM part number for the bottle on the parts counter is 08885-02506 (also packaged as 08885-02906 in some regions). Both are the same fluid. The older 4th-gen owner's manual originally listed 75W-90 for some build years, but Toyota's current TSB and the 2010+ spec moved everything to 75W-85 \u2014 using 75W-85 is correct across 2003-2024 4Runners. A name-brand synthetic 75W-90 GL-5 like Valvoline SynPower works fine if the parts house is out of OEM.\n\nCapacity is 1.2 to 1.3 liters (1.3 to 1.4 quarts). The right approach is the same as every other diff: pump until it runs out the fill hole, then stop. Don't try to hit a specific number \u2014 vehicle level matters more than the spec sheet.\n\nBoth plugs are 24mm hex on most years (some 4th-gen use a 10mm internal hex). They're located on the front cover of the diff. The fill plug is the higher one; the drain plug is below it. Always crack the fill plug FIRST. A stuck fill plug after you've drained means a tow.\n\nService interval is the part most owners get wrong. Toyota's normal-service interval reads as \"inspect\" rather than \"replace,\" which gets read as \"do nothing.\" The severe service schedule \u2014 which applies to anyone who tows, wheels in dust, or drives in stop-and-go \u2014 calls for 30,000 miles. If you wheel in water at all, change it after every deep crossing; water in a differential is fast destruction.","steps":["Park on level ground. Drive a few miles to warm the fluid for cleaner drainage.","Slide drain pan under the front diff.","Crack the FILL plug first (24mm hex, upper plug on front cover). If it won't break loose, stop.","Remove the drain plug (lower plug). Let it drain 15\u201320 minutes.","Inspect drained fluid. Dark brown is normal aging. Milky means water \u2014 investigate seals and the breather tube. Metal flakes mean ring-and-pinion wear.","Install new crush gasket on drain plug. Torque to 36 ft-lb.","Use a fluid pump to add 75W-85 GL-5 through the fill hole. Stop when it dribbles out steady.","Install fill plug with new crush gasket. Torque to 36 ft-lb.","Wipe down, check for leaks after a short drive."],"pros":["Cheap maintenance that prevents expensive ring-and-pinion damage","Fluid condition gives early warning of pinion bearing wear or water intrusion","OEM fluid is available at any Toyota dealer for under $20/quart","Same 75W-85 spec covers front and rear \u2014 buy 3 quarts, do both diffs"],"cons":["24mm hex requires a specific bit \u2014 not in every starter socket set","No drain plug magnet from the factory on most years \u2014 aftermarket upgrade recommended","Severe service interval is genuinely needed for off-road and tow rigs, not just marketing fine print"]},{"id":"4runner-oil-change-4runner","title":"Oil Change \u2014 1GR-FE 4.0L V6","vehicle":"4runner","category":"maintenance","subcategory":"engine_oil","type":"task","difficulty":1,"time_hours":{"low":0.5,"high":1},"cost_usd":{"low":40,"high":80},"tools_required":["14mm socket","64mm oil filter cap socket","drain pan","torque wrench","shop rags"],"skills_required":["basic wrenching"],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2003-2009","2010-2024"],"tags":["oil-change","1gr-fe","4.0l","maintenance"],"parts":[{"name":"0W-20 full synthetic (6.4 qt 2010+ / 5.5 qt 4th gen)","part_number":"08880-83865","vendor":"Toyota / API SP equivalent","price_estimate_usd":35},{"name":"Toyota cartridge oil filter kit","part_number":"04152-YZZA1","vendor":"Toyota","price_estimate_usd":8},{"name":"Drain plug gasket (aluminum washer)","part_number":"90430-12031","vendor":"Toyota","price_estimate_usd":1}],"citations":[{"title":"Toyota 1GR-FE 4.0L service info \u2014 oil capacity and cartridge filter spec","url":"https://techinfo.toyota.com"},{"title":"4Runner forum drain plug torque (27 ft-lb) and filter housing torque (18 ft-lb)","url":"https://www.toyota-4runner.org"}],"alternatives":[],"summary":"The 1GR-FE 4.0L V6 holds 6.4 quarts of 0W-20 (5th gen 2010+) or 5.5 quarts of 5W-30 (4th gen 2003\u20132009). Cartridge filter is on the bottom of the engine with a built-in drain valve. Drain plug torque 27 ft-lb, filter housing 18 ft-lb. Job takes 30\u201360 minutes.","description":"The 1GR-FE V6 powers every 4Runner from 4th gen (2003) through 5th gen (2010\u20132024). The 4th gen used 5W-30 conventional or blend; the 5th gen switched to 0W-20 full synthetic for fuel economy. Either works mechanically \u2014 match the manual to keep warranty and CARB OBD-II monitors happy.\n\n**Cartridge filter design.** Toyota's cartridge filter sits on the bottom of the engine, behind the front skid plate. It has a clever drain valve \u2014 thread a small plastic drain tool (included in the Toyota filter kit) into the bottom of the housing first, and oil drains out through a hose you can route into your pan. This keeps oil off the skid plate. Without the drain tool, oil sprays everywhere when you crack the housing.\n\n**Cap socket.** 64mm 14-flute oil filter cap socket fits both gens. Cheap aluminum versions strip teeth out of the cap \u2014 buy the steel version (~$15 at any parts store) once and keep it for life.\n\n**Skid plate access.** Stock front skid plate has an oval cutout that gives you access to the filter and drain plug without dropping the skid. Aftermarket skids (Rago Fabrication, RCI, BAMF) keep the access port; bare-bones plate-style skids don't, and you'll have to drop them. Plan accordingly.\n\n**Oil choice.** 0W-20 full synthetic is correct for 5th gen. Mobil 1 EP, Pennzoil Platinum, Valvoline EP, and Toyota TGMO branded oils are all SN+/SP-rated and work fine. For high-mileage 4Runners (200K+) some owners step up to 5W-30 to quiet a slightly noisy timing chain \u2014 Toyota's TSB allows this in hot climates without warranty implications.","steps":["Run engine 2\u20133 minutes to warm and thin the oil. Shut off.","Lift the front of the truck or work over ramps. The skid plate access port should align with the filter housing.","Position drain pan under the drain plug (rear of oil pan). Remove 14mm drain plug. Drain 5+ minutes.","While draining, thread the small plastic drain tool (from the Toyota filter kit) into the bottom of the filter housing. Oil will drain through it into your pan.","Once filter housing is drained, use the 64mm cap socket to remove the housing cover. Lift out the old cartridge.","Wipe the housing clean. Replace both O-rings on the cover (one large, one small for the drain valve plug).","Snap the new cartridge into the cover. Reinstall, torque to 18 ft-lb (25 Nm). Do not exceed \u2014 the aluminum housing strips.","Remove the drain tool from the housing. Install the small drain valve plug with its new O-ring. Snug, no torque spec, do not overtighten.","Install drain plug with new aluminum crush washer. Torque to 27 ft-lb (37 Nm).","Add 6.4 qt (5th gen) or 5.5 qt (4th gen) of correct viscosity oil through the valve cover fill cap.","Start engine. Verify oil pressure light goes out within 3 seconds. Idle 30 seconds.","Shut off, wait 5 minutes, check dipstick. Top off as needed."],"pros":["Drain tool keeps oil off everything","100K timing chain (no belt to worry about)","Bulletproof 1GR-FE \u2014 300K is the expected service life","Cartridge filter is environmentally friendlier than spin-on"],"cons":["Cap socket is a special tool ($15)","O-ring forgotten on the drain valve = leak after 100 miles","Aluminum housing strips threads if overtightened","Aftermarket skid plates may block filter access"]},{"id":"4runner-rear-diff-fluid","title":"Rear Differential Fluid Change","vehicle":"4runner","category":"maintenance","type":"task","difficulty":2,"time_hours":{"low":0.5,"high":1.5},"cost_usd":{"low":35,"high":80},"tools_required":["24mm socket","10mm hex bit (some years)","fluid pump","drain pan","torque wrench"],"skills_required":["basic wrenching","fluid handling"],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","mild_trail","moderate_trail","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2003-2009","2010-2024"],"tags":["axles","fluids","75w-85","gl-5"],"parts":[{"name":"Toyota Genuine 75W-85 GL-5 Gear Oil (quart)","part_number":"08885-02506","vendor":"Toyota","price_estimate_usd":18},{"name":"Valvoline SynPower 75W-90 GL-5 (acceptable substitute)","vendor":"AutoZone/RockAuto","price_estimate_usd":13},{"name":"Drain plug gasket","part_number":"90430-24003","vendor":"Toyota","price_estimate_usd":2},{"name":"Fill plug gasket","part_number":"12157-10010","vendor":"Toyota","price_estimate_usd":2}],"citations":[{"title":"Trail4R \u2014 5th gen driveline fluids","url":"https://trail4runner.com/2018/12/24/4runner-driveline-fluids-how-and-when-to-change-them/"},{"title":"The Track Ahead \u2014 4th gen diff fluid replacement","url":"https://thetrackahead.com/projects/4th-gen-toyota-4runner/front-and-rear-differential-fluid-replacement-4th-gen-toyota-4runner/"}],"alternatives":[],"summary":"Toyota wants 75W-85 GL-5 in the rear diff of every 4th and 5th gen 4Runner; capacity is around 3.0 to 3.2 quarts, and the service is a 30-minute job once the rig is on jack stands.","description":"The 4Runner rear differential is a wet-pinion housing under a stamped steel cover (no removable cover on most years, drain and fill plugs only). On 4th gen (2003\u20132009) the rear takes about 3.2 quarts and is open or factory locked depending on trim. On 5th gen (2010+) the rear diff on TRD Off-Road and TRD Pro models has a factory electric locker; on SR5 and Limited it's open or fitted with an Auto LSD that uses brake pressure to mimic limited slip \u2014 neither of these requires a friction additive.\n\nThe factory fluid spec is 75W-85 GL-5. Toyota sells it under part number 08885-02506. The challenge is finding it locally \u2014 most parts stores stock 75W-90 GL-5 instead. 75W-90 GL-5 is fully acceptable for a 4Runner that doesn't tow heavy. If you tow at GVWR or live where summer pavement bakes, the slightly thicker 75W-90 is arguably better. Stick with synthetic from a reputable brand: Valvoline, Mobil 1, Amsoil, Royal Purple, or Toyota.\n\nThe fill plug (24mm) sits on the rear face of the diff housing. The drain plug (24mm) sits at the bottom. Both use crush gaskets (parts 12157-10010 and 90430-24003) \u2014 replace them at every service, they're $2 each. Open the fill first to confirm it isn't seized. Drain. Reinstall drain at 36 ft-lbs. Pump fresh gear oil into the fill hole until it begins to overflow at the threshold of the port. Reinstall fill at 36 ft-lbs.\n\nWhile you're there, check the rear breather hose for cracks or pinches \u2014 water crossings push water up the breather faster than past the seals. If your 4Runner sees water above the tire tops, plan to drain the diff within a week and look for milky contamination.","steps":["Drive the rig 10 minutes to warm the gear oil.","Park level on jack stands; chock the front wheels.","Position a drain pan under the diff.","Remove the fill plug first (24mm). Verify it's loose before you drain.","Remove the drain plug (24mm). Let the diff drain fully.","Inspect the magnet on the drain plug for metal debris.","Install a fresh gasket on the drain plug. Torque to 36 ft-lbs.","Pump 75W-85 (or 75W-90) into the fill hole until it overflows.","Install a fresh gasket on the fill plug. Torque to 36 ft-lbs.","Inspect the breather hose for damage while you're under the rig."],"pros":["30\u201360 minutes start to finish","Almost no specialized tools \u2014 one socket and a pump","Catches water intrusion and bearing failure early","Cheap insurance for an axle that's already $2,000+ to replace"],"cons":["75W-85 OEM fluid is hard to find locally \u2014 usually online order","No drain pan port on the underside means floor jack and stands are required","Crush gaskets must be replaced or you'll get a slow leak"]},{"id":"4runner-spark-plugs-4runner","title":"Spark Plug Replacement \u2014 1GR-FE 4.0L","vehicle":"4runner","category":"maintenance","type":"task","difficulty":3,"time_hours":{"low":2,"high":4},"cost_usd":{"low":80,"high":160},"tools_required":["3/8\" ratchet","10mm socket","long extension","5/8\" spark plug socket","torque wrench","T20 Torx"],"skills_required":["basic wrenching","intake removal patience"],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2003-2009","2010-2024"],"tags":["ignition","preventative","1GR-FE"],"parts":[{"name":"Denso K20HR-U11 nickel (set of 6) \u2014 OEM","part_number":"90919-01235","vendor":"Toyota / Denso","price_estimate_usd":45},{"name":"NGK LFR6C11 nickel (set of 6) \u2014 OEM alt","part_number":"90919-01236","vendor":"Toyota / NGK","price_estimate_usd":42},{"name":"Denso SK20HR11 iridium long-life (set of 6) \u2014 extended interval","part_number":"3438","vendor":"Denso","price_estimate_usd":90},{"name":"Dielectric grease","vendor":"Permatex","price_estimate_usd":4}],"citations":[{"title":"TheTrackAhead \u2014 4th gen 4Runner spark plug guide","url":"https://thetrackahead.com/projects/4th-gen-toyota-4runner/spark-plug-replacement-1gre-fe-40l-v6-4th-gen-toyota-4runner/"},{"title":"Toyota-4runner.org \u2014 1GR-FE plug change instructions","url":"https://www.toyota-4runner.org/maintenance-detailing/57478-4-0-v6-1gr-fe-spark-plug-change-instructions.html"},{"title":"ToyotaNation \u2014 Changing plugs on the 1GR-FE","url":"https://www.toyotanation.com/threads/changing-the-spark-plugs-on-the-1gr-fe-4-0-v6.378215/"}],"alternatives":[],"summary":"The 1GR-FE V6 calls for nickel plugs at 30,000 miles. You can run Denso iridium long-life plugs to push the interval out, but plan on pulling the intake plenum to reach the rear three plugs on either choice.","description":"The 1GR-FE 4.0L V6 in the 4th and 5th gen 4Runner has been one of Toyota's most reliable engines, but Toyota specced it with nickel-copper plugs and a short 30,000-mile interval. That short interval is the price you pay for getting at the rear three plugs \u2014 they sit underneath the upper intake plenum, and reaching them means removing six plenum bolts, the throttle body harness, and the PCV hose. Many owners just buy iridium long-life plugs once and skip the chore for the next 100,000 miles.\n\nOEM front-three plugs are Denso K20HR-U11 (Toyota 90919-01235) or NGK LFR6C11 (Toyota 90919-01236) \u2014 both nickel. The factory-equivalent iridium upgrade is Denso SK20HR11 (Denso #3438), which fits identically and runs roughly 100,000 miles. Gap spec is 0.043\" (1.1mm) \u2014 verify with a feeler gauge on every new plug, even iridium pre-gapped sets, because shipping drops happen.\n\nThe front three plugs are easy: pop the coil pack cover, unbolt each coil with a 10mm, lift it out, and pull the plug with a 5/8\" deep socket on a long extension. Twenty minutes total. The rear three are the actual job. Sequence matters: disconnect the throttle body connectors, the EVAP line, the PCV hose, the brake booster vacuum hose, and unbolt the upper plenum (six 12mm bolts). Lift the plenum off, set it aside on a clean shop rag, and inspect the gasket \u2014 if it is the original rubberized gasket, replace it now (Toyota 17171-31040, about $35).\n\nTorque the plugs to 13 ft-lb (17.5 N\u00b7m). The 1GR-FE uses 14mm plugs, but the same caution applies as any aluminum head \u2014 go past 18 ft-lb and you risk pulling threads. No anti-seize on the plug threads (Denso and NGK both say not to). A dab of dielectric grease in each coil boot keeps the boot from sticking the next time.\n\nReinstall the plenum with a fresh gasket if you pulled the old one. Hit each bolt in a crisscross pattern at 18 ft-lb. Reconnect every harness, hose, and clip you removed. Disconnect the battery for 5 minutes before reconnecting to force the ECU to relearn idle on first start.","steps":["Cold engine. Disconnect negative battery cable.","Remove the engine cover (pulls up off rubber grommets).","Front three plugs first: unbolt each coil pack (10mm), lift coil, pull plug with 5/8\" socket on a long extension.","Verify gap (0.043\"), install new plug hand-tight, torque to 13 ft-lb.","Reinstall coil with dielectric grease in the boot.","Rear three: disconnect throttle body connectors, EVAP line, PCV hose, brake booster line.","Unbolt the upper plenum (six 12mm bolts). Lift off. Cover the lower intake openings with rags.","Repeat coil-and-plug procedure on cylinders 2, 4, 6.","Reinstall plenum with fresh gasket if needed. Torque bolts in a crisscross pattern to 18 ft-lb.","Reconnect all hoses, sensors, and harnesses. Reconnect battery. Start, listen for vacuum leaks at the plenum gasket."],"pros":["Iridium upgrade pushes the next service to 100,000 miles","Restores smooth idle and fuel economy on a high-mileage 1GR-FE","Catches a leaking plenum gasket while you have the intake apart","Skill builds your confidence for bigger intake jobs later"],"cons":["Rear three plugs require intake plenum removal","14mm aluminum head threads strip if you over-torque","Plenum gasket may need replacement if original (extra $35)","3\u20134 hour first-time job \u2014 set a full afternoon aside"]},{"id":"4runner-transfer-case-fluid-4runner","title":"Transfer Case Fluid Change","vehicle":"4runner","category":"maintenance","type":"task","difficulty":2,"time_hours":{"low":0.5,"high":1},"cost_usd":{"low":25,"high":45},"tools_required":["24mm socket","1/2 ratchet","fluid pump","drain pan","torque wrench"],"skills_required":["basic wrenching"],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2010-2024"],"tags":["fluids","drivetrain","preventive"],"parts":[{"name":"Toyota Genuine 75W Transfer Case Fluid (1 quart)","part_number":"08885-81080","vendor":"Toyota","price_estimate_usd":18},{"name":"Toyota Drain Plug Gasket (crush washer)","part_number":"12157-10010","vendor":"Toyota","price_estimate_usd":2}],"citations":[{"title":"Blau Parts \u2014 Toyota 4Runner Transfer Case Fluid Types","url":"https://www.blauparts.com/blog/what-type-of-transfer-case-fluid-does-my-toyota-4runner-take.html"},{"title":"5th gen 4Runner transfer case oil \u2014 BobIsTheOilGuy","url":"https://bobistheoilguy.com/forums/threads/5th-gen-4runner-transfer-case-oil.396812/"}],"alternatives":[],"summary":"5th gen 4Runners (2010\u20132024) take 1.4 liters of Toyota's 75W transfer case fluid \u2014 part 08885-81080. Plan on 30 minutes, two crush washers, and a fluid pump.","description":"Toyota's recommended service interval is 60,000 miles for severe-use and never for normal-use \u2014 meaning if you ever tow, drive dusty roads, hit dirt, or live in Phoenix, you fall under severe service. Real-world: change it at 60k regardless. The factory fill is dark amber when new and looks like burnt coffee when worn. The 5th gen V8 RX is gone \u2014 this guide covers the 4.0L 1GR-FE with the VF4BM transfer case found in SR5, TRD Off-Road, TRD Pro, and Limited.\n\nThe critical part-number note: the 5th gen takes 75W transfer case fluid, not 75W-90 gear oil. Toyota changed the spec around 2010 to a lighter fluid to reduce drivetrain drag. Part 08885-81080 (sometimes labeled 08885-01806) is the correct fill. Old-school internet advice telling you to use 75W-90 is leftover from 3rd and 4th gen 4Runners. Use the right fluid \u2014 your case will thank you.\n\nPull the 4Runner up onto level ground (ramps or jack stands; do not work on a slope or you'll over- or under-fill). The transfer case sits behind the transmission, easily accessible from below. The fill plug is on the driver's side, upper position, 24mm hex head. The drain plug is on the bottom, also 24mm. Crack the fill plug first \u2014 same rule as every other case fluid job. If the fill won't open, you don't want to find that out after the drain is empty on your driveway.\n\nDrain into a clean catch pan so you can inspect the fluid. Light metallic shimmer is normal, chunks are not. Install a fresh crush washer on the drain plug (don't reuse the old one; it won't seal right) and torque to 27 ft-lb. Pump 1.4 liters / 1.47 quarts of fresh 75W through the fill hole \u2014 the case is full when fluid trickles out the fill port. Install the fill plug with a new crush washer at 27 ft-lb. Check for leaks after a short drive.","steps":["Park on level ground. Get the 4Runner up on ramps or jack stands.","Locate the transfer case behind the transmission. Identify fill plug (driver side, upper) and drain plug (bottom).","Crack the fill plug loose first with a 24mm socket. If it won't open, stop and address.","Position drain pan, remove drain plug, let fluid drain fully (~10 min).","Inspect drained fluid for metal chunks or unusual contamination.","Install fresh crush washer on drain plug. Torque to 27 ft-lb.","Pump fresh Toyota 75W (part 08885-81080) into fill hole until fluid trickles back out \u2014 about 1.4 liters.","Install fill plug with new crush washer. Torque to 27 ft-lb.","Lower the vehicle, start engine, run through 4H and 4L to circulate.","Park on level again and re-check the fill level after 5 minutes."],"pros":["30-minute job; transfer case rebuilds run $1,500+","Toyota OE fluid is widely available from any Toyota dealer","Crush washers are cheap and prevent slow weeps"],"cons":["Wrong fluid (75W-90 instead of 75W) increases drag and can affect mileage","Aluminum case threads strip if you over-torque the plugs","Reusing crush washers is a guaranteed slow leak"]},{"id":"4runner-recovery-gear-kit","title":"Recovery Gear Starter Kit for the 4Runner","vehicle":"4runner","category":"recovery","type":"kit","difficulty":1,"time_hours":{"low":1,"high":2},"cost_usd":{"low":250,"high":600},"tools_required":["none required for kit assembly"],"skills_required":["basic recovery technique","rigging awareness"],"build_tiers":["mild_trail","moderate_trail","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2010-2024"],"tags":["recovery","kinetic-rope","snatch-strap","traction-boards","hi-lift"],"parts":[{"name":"ARB Recovery Bag Kit (kinetic rope, snatch block, tree saver, gloves)","vendor":"ARB 4x4 Accessories","price_estimate_usd":499},{"name":"Bubba Rope 20' 7/8\" Gator-Jaw Kinetic Recovery Rope","vendor":"Bubba Rope","price_estimate_usd":119},{"name":"Maxtrax MKII Traction Boards (pair)","vendor":"Maxtrax","price_estimate_usd":349},{"name":"Rhino USA 3/4\" Forged D-Ring Shackles (pair)","vendor":"Rhino USA","price_estimate_usd":29}],"citations":[{"title":"ARB Recovery Gear Overview","url":"https://www.arbusa.com"},{"title":"Maxtrax MKII Product Page","url":"https://www.maxtrax.com.au"}],"alternatives":["4runner-winch","4runner-front-bumper","4runner-rear-bumper"],"summary":"A kinetic rope, D-rings, traction boards, and a snatch block cover most trail recovery situations \u2014 and the 4Runner's factory front tow hooks are genuine recovery points, which simplifies the attachment question compared to some other IFS platforms.","description":"The 5th gen 4Runner includes factory tow hooks in the front that are frame-mounted, painted in body color, and often overlooked. These are functional recovery points for straight-ahead pull recovery with a kinetic rope. Attach a D-ring shackle (3/4\" rated, like the Rhino USA units) to the factory hook eye and you have a legitimate front attachment point \u2014 no steel bumper required for basic recoveries. For rear recovery, the OEM receiver hitch is the correct attachment point; use a hitch-mount D-ring receiver shackle to connect a rope.\n\nThe kinetic rope is the most important item in any recovery kit. Bubba Rope's Gator-Jaw 7/8\" 20' kinetic rope stores energy during extension and releases it as a smooth forward pull \u2014 far more effective than a static tow strap and much gentler on the recovery points. Static straps shock-load the anchor points on a stuck vehicle; kinetic ropes don't. A snatch block doubles the pull force when rigged as a mechanical advantage system \u2014 essential for deep mud or heavy-vehicle recoveries where a single vehicle pull isn't enough.\n\nMaxtrax traction boards are the self-recovery go-to for sand, mud, and snow. Dig out the wheel, slide the board under the drive tire, and drive off it. The 4Runner's locked rear differential (on Trail and TRD Pro trims) combined with Maxtrax turns single-vehicle self-recovery into a reliable procedure even in soft terrain. If your 4Runner is an SR5 or Limited without a locker, Maxtrax plus controlled throttle will still get you out of most situations where only one wheel is buried.\n\nKeep the recovery kit in a dedicated bag or box where it can be reached without unloading the cargo area. ARB's recovery bag includes everything in a single roll-top bag with organized pockets for each item \u2014 a practical solution for 4Runners where the cargo floor is already occupied by gear.","steps":["Verify front tow hook condition \u2014 inspect for cracks or deformation before relying on them","Obtain hitch-mount D-ring receiver shackle for rear recovery point if steel bumper is not installed","Store kinetic rope and snatch block in ARB or similar recovery bag, accessible from rear hatch without full unloading","Mount Maxtrax boards to roof rack or exterior mount where they can be accessed when the truck is stuck \u2014 not buried under gear","Carry two D-ring shackles at minimum \u2014 one for the front and one for the rear simultaneously","Practice a vehicle-to-vehicle kinetic rope recovery in a low-stakes situation before relying on the technique","After any recovery: inspect rope for cuts, check shackles for deformation, dry out gear before storing"],"pros":["Factory front tow hooks on the 4Runner are genuine frame-mounted recovery points \u2014 eliminates the bumper prerequisite for basic recovery","ARB Recovery Bag bundles kinetic rope, snatch block, tree saver, and gloves in one organized kit","Maxtrax boards enable self-recovery \u2014 valuable for solo overland trips where a second vehicle isn't available","Kinetic rope is safer and more effective than static tow straps in vehicle-to-vehicle recovery","Compact kit \u2014 the full setup fits in a medium bag under the rear cargo floor"],"cons":["Factory tow hooks are for straight pulls only \u2014 angled recovery loads can bend the hook eye","Rear recovery requires a hitch-mount shackle without a steel bumper \u2014 a weak link if the recovery involves a hard pull","Maxtrax boards are expensive for what they are; cheaper traction boards exist but with lower grip and durability","Without a locker (SR5/Limited), traction boards require correct throttle technique to work \u2014 recovery is not automatic","Recovery gear provides no value without operator technique \u2014 learn proper kinetic rope handling before the first trail trip"]},{"id":"4runner-winch","title":"Winch for the 4Runner \u2014 10,000 lb Minimum","vehicle":"4runner","category":"recovery","type":"part","difficulty":2,"time_hours":{"low":1,"high":2},"cost_usd":{"low":349,"high":1200},"tools_required":["socket set","torque wrench","wire cutters/stripper","multimeter","drill and bits"],"skills_required":["basic wrenching","basic electrical wiring"],"build_tiers":["mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2010-2024"],"tags":["recovery","winch","self-recovery","synthetic-rope","waterproof"],"parts":[{"name":"Warn VR EVO 10-S Winch (10,000 lb, synthetic rope)","vendor":"Warn","price_estimate_usd":449},{"name":"Smittybilt X20 10K Winch (10,000 lb, synthetic rope)","vendor":"Smittybilt","price_estimate_usd":349},{"name":"Warn M10000-S Winch (10,000 lb, synthetic rope)","vendor":"Warn","price_estimate_usd":749},{"name":"Warn Zeon 10-S Winch (10,000 lb, synthetic rope)","vendor":"Warn","price_estimate_usd":1099}],"citations":[{"title":"Warn Winch Selection Guide","url":"https://www.warn.com"},{"title":"Smittybilt X20 10K Product Page","url":"https://www.smittybilt.com"}],"alternatives":["4runner-front-bumper","4runner-recovery-gear-kit"],"summary":"The 4Runner's GVWR of 4,750\u20135,300 lbs puts the minimum winch rating at 10,000 lbs \u2014 and a steel front bumper with an integrated winch plate is required infrastructure before the winch itself can be safely installed.","description":"The 1.5x GVWR rule puts the 4Runner firmly at 10,000 lbs minimum line pull for the winch. The Warn VR EVO 10-S is the correct choice for most 4Runner builds: it's a proven IP68-rated waterproof motor, uses Warn's quality control standard, runs 10,000 lbs of line pull on synthetic rope, and the price point is realistic. The Warn Zeon 10-S is the premium alternative \u2014 machined aluminum fairlead, faster line speed, and better heat dissipation for prolonged use. For rock crawling where the winch may run multiple full-drum cycles in a session, the Zeon's thermal capacity matters.\n\nWaterproofing ratings matter for the 4Runner more than for some other applications. The 4Runner is commonly used in stream crossings and muddy terrain. IP67 or IP68 sealing on the winch motor and control box is the specification to verify \u2014 lower ratings can allow water ingress during repeated water crossings, which damages the motor over time. The Warn VR EVO, M10000-S, and Zeon series all meet IP68. The Smittybilt X20 is rated IP67 \u2014 one step down \u2014 and the control box is the more vulnerable component to watch.\n\nRemote vs in-cab control: every winch on this list includes a wireless remote. Using the remote from a safe distance is the correct operating procedure \u2014 stand clear of the synthetic rope line and operate from a 90\u00b0 angle to the pull direction where possible. Route the hardwired in-cab control lead for a backup control option.\n\nSteel front bumper with winch plate is a firm prerequisite. The 4Runner OEM bumper has no winch mounting provision. The load path from the winch through the bumper to the frame is the structural element that makes the recovery work \u2014 a winch bolted to a body-mounted plate or bolted through the plastic bumper skin is a failure waiting to happen under load.","steps":["Install steel front bumper with integrated winch plate before ordering winch","Confirm winch plate dimensions match winch (most use 10\" x 4.5\" SAE standard pattern)","Mount winch to winch plate; torque all four mounting bolts to manufacturer spec","Route positive cable from winch to battery; do not connect to battery terminal yet","Route negative cable to chassis ground or battery negative with clean connection point","Route control harness into cab; mount in-cab switch or store wireless remote accessibly","Connect positive cable to battery terminal last","Test winch in both directions with no load; verify all control options function","Spool out full rope under light tension to seat rope evenly on drum","Test pull against a fixed anchor before trusting the system in the field"],"pros":["Warn VR EVO 10-S is the benchmark mid-range winch \u2014 IP68 rated, reliable, backed by Warn's warranty and parts availability","Wireless remote standard on all listed models \u2014 operate from a safe distance from the line","Snatch block doubles effective pull to 20,000 lbs for heavy recoveries","Synthetic rope is lighter, safer, and easier to handle than steel cable","Warn Zeon 10-S is worth the premium for rock crawling builds that will run the winch hard"],"cons":["Steel front bumper is a mandatory prerequisite \u2014 total winch system cost is winch + bumper ($1,100\u2013$2,800 combined)","Smittybilt X20 is adequate for occasional trail recovery but has a weaker motor and less robust internals than Warn \u2014 not recommended for frequent heavy use","Winch adds 60\u201390 lbs to the front axle \u2014 check front axle gross weight if the build is heavily loaded","Synthetic rope can be cut by sharp metal edges during recovery \u2014 use a rope protector sleeve at any contact point","In-cab wiring at 400\u2013500 amp draw requires proper gauge wire (2/0 AWG minimum) \u2014 undersized wire is a fire hazard"]},{"id":"4runner-tie-rods-4runner","title":"Tie Rod Replacement","vehicle":"4runner","category":"steering","type":"task","difficulty":3,"time_hours":{"low":2,"high":4},"cost_usd":{"low":80,"high":350},"tools_required":["22mm socket","19mm wrench","tie rod removal tool","torque wrench","grease gun","pickle fork or puller"],"skills_required":["basic wrenching","alignment understanding"],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2010-2024"],"tags":["steering","alignment","wear-item","5th-gen"],"parts":[{"name":"Toyota OEM inner tie rod","part_number":"45503-39395","vendor":"Toyota","price_estimate_usd":95},{"name":"Moog outer tie rod (greasable)","part_number":"ES80895","vendor":"Moog","price_estimate_usd":45},{"name":"555 Japan outer tie rod","part_number":"SE-T291L","vendor":"555","price_estimate_usd":38},{"name":"Apex Chassis HD tie rod kit","vendor":"Apex Chassis","price_estimate_usd":320}],"citations":[{"title":"How To Change Inner and Outer Tie Rods on 5th Gen 4Runner \u2014 Trail4Runner","url":"https://trail4runner.com/2020/02/01/how-to-change-inner-and-outer-tie-rods-on-5th-gen-4runner/"},{"title":"Apex Chassis Tie Rod Kit Install \u2014 Trail4Runner","url":"https://trail4runner.com/2024/12/05/apex-chassis-tie-rod-kit-install-5th-gen/"}],"alternatives":[],"summary":"If your 5th gen 4Runner wanders, clunks over bumps, or shows uneven tire wear at 80\u2013120k miles, the outer tie rods are usually the cause \u2014 replace in pairs, use OEM for inners, and get an alignment the same day.","description":"Tie rods on the 5th gen 4Runner are a wear item. Outer tie rod ends carry the steering load through a ball-and-socket joint, and the rubber boot is the only thing protecting that joint from dirt and water. Boot tears, water gets in, the ball goes dry, the joint develops play, and the steering loses its center. Expect to replace outers somewhere between 80,000 and 130,000 miles on a stock truck \u2014 sooner if you wheel hard or run oversized tires.\n\nThe diagnosis is straightforward. Jack the front, grab the tire at 3 and 9 o'clock, and shake side to side. Any clunk or play from the steering linkage points to the tie rods. Watch the outer joint as someone rocks the steering wheel \u2014 visible movement in the ball joint means it is worn out. Uneven tire wear, especially feathering on the inside edge, is the long-term symptom of a misaligned front end driven on bad tie rods.\n\nParts choice matters more than usual on this truck. For the outer, Moog ES80895 is the standard aftermarket choice and is greasable, which lets you extend its service life with regular maintenance. The 555 brand (made in Japan, OEM supplier to many Toyota platforms) is the no-grease quality match for the factory part at a lower price than dealer. The Toyota OEM outer is excellent but expensive. For the inner, go OEM (45503-39395) \u2014 Toyota inners run forever, while aftermarket inners are the most common cause of premature replacement on this platform. If you wheel hard or run armor and lockers, the Apex Chassis kit at $320 uses heim joints and a larger-diameter forged rod that survives rock contact and heavy steering loads.\n\nThe replacement itself is straightforward but earns its difficulty rating from rust. The outer tie rod threads onto the inner with a jam nut, and on any truck that has lived in the salt belt, that jam nut and the boot clamp on the inner can be seized. Plan for penetrating oil overnight before you start. Count threads or measure the assembled length before disassembly \u2014 that measurement becomes your baseline toe setting and gets you home for the alignment shop.","steps":["Measure the existing assembled tie rod length from the jam nut to the end of the outer ball joint. Record it.","Loosen the lug nuts, raise the front, support on jack stands, remove the wheel.","Loosen the jam nut between the inner and outer tie rod. Penetrating oil overnight if it is seized.","Remove the 22mm castle nut from the outer tie rod stud. Separate the outer from the steering knuckle with a tie rod puller or pickle fork.","Count and record the number of turns as you spin the outer off the inner. Replace with the new outer and thread it on the same number of turns.","For the inner, remove the boot clamp, slide the boot back, and use a tie rod tool to break the inner free from the steering rack. Thread the new inner in and torque to 60 ft-lbs.","Slide the boot back over, clamp, then reinstall the outer and the steering knuckle. Torque the castle nut to 67 ft-lbs and the jam nut to 65 ft-lbs.","Reinstall the wheel, lower the truck, and drive directly to an alignment shop. Do not skip the alignment."],"pros":["Restores steering feel and centerline immediately","Eliminates the clunk over bumps","OEM inner plus greasable outer is the durability sweet spot","Apex HD kit handles trail abuse if you wheel often"],"cons":["Inner tie rod tool ($30) is needed if you do not already own one","Salt-belt trucks fight the jam nut and boot clamp","Alignment is mandatory afterward \u2014 budget another $100","Cheap aftermarket inners fail in 20\u201330k miles"]},{"id":"4runner-budget-lift","title":"Budget Lift Kit \u2014 Bilstein 5100 and Spacers (\u2264$600)","vehicle":"4runner","category":"suspension","type":"kit","difficulty":2,"time_hours":{"low":3,"high":6},"cost_usd":{"low":399,"high":649},"tools_required":["floor jack","jack stands","socket set","torque wrench","spring compressor","breaker bar"],"skills_required":["suspension work","basic wrenching","torque specs"],"build_tiers":["mild_trail","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2010-2024"],"tags":["lift","suspension","budget","5100","spacer","non-KDSS"],"parts":[{"name":"Bilstein B8 5100 Front and Rear Set (5th Gen 4Runner 2010-2024)","vendor":"Bilstein","price_estimate_usd":549},{"name":"Icon Vehicle Dynamics Stage 1 Lift System (spacer, 5th Gen 4Runner)","vendor":"Icon Vehicle Dynamics","price_estimate_usd":399},{"name":"Rough Country 2\" Lift Kit (5th Gen 4Runner, non-KDSS)","vendor":"Rough Country","price_estimate_usd":299}],"citations":[{"title":"Bilstein 5100 Product Specifications","url":"https://www.bilstein.com/us/en/"},{"title":"4Runner World Lift Kit Discussion","url":"https://www.4runner.org"}],"alternatives":["4runner-mid-lift","4runner-expedition-lift"],"summary":"The Bilstein B8 5100 set is the correct budget lift for non-KDSS 4Runners \u2014 a direct replacement of the factory shocks that delivers 1.5\"\u20132\" of lift and meaningfully better damping without touching the suspension geometry.","description":"The 5th gen 4Runner (2010\u20132024) comes in two suspension configurations: non-KDSS (SR5, Limited, and base trims) and KDSS-equipped (Trail Edition, TRD Pro, and some Limited packages). KDSS stands for Kinetic Dynamic Suspension System \u2014 a hydraulic sway bar linkage that passively adjusts front-to-rear roll resistance for trail articulation. Standard coilovers and spacer lifts are not compatible with KDSS because the KDSS system requires specific shock valving to function correctly. Before ordering any budget lift, confirm whether your 4Runner has KDSS by looking under the front and rear for hydraulic cylinders connecting the sway bar end links to the axle. Non-KDSS 4Runners have a conventional mechanical sway bar.\n\nFor non-KDSS 4Runners, the Bilstein B8 5100 is the cleanest budget approach. The 5100 is a monotube shock with a height adjustment collar on the front units \u2014 set to roughly 1.75\" of lift front and use the preload-adjusted rear to match. You get improved damping, the lift, and no geometry compromise at this height. The Icon Stage 1 spacer lift is an alternative that uses a urethane spacer above the OEM spring \u2014 it lifts by 1.5\"\u20132\" but keeps the factory shock, which is fine if the factory dampers still have life. The Rough Country kit is the lowest-cost option but uses lower-quality dampers and is not recommended for builds that will see regular trail use.\n\nAt 2\" of lift on a non-KDSS 4Runner, the factory UCAs are operating at their outer geometry limit but don't yet require replacement. Caster changes are manageable. For anything above 2\", UCAs become necessary on the 4Runner just as on the Tacoma.","steps":["Confirm KDSS or non-KDSS configuration before ordering \u2014 this lift path is for non-KDSS only","Lift front of vehicle, support on jack stands, remove front wheels","Remove OEM front shock/strut assembly (disconnect sway bar end link, brake line bracket, strut-to-knuckle bolts, upper mount)","Install Bilstein 5100 coilover; set height collar to desired lift per Bilstein's height chart","Torque strut-to-knuckle and upper mount to spec","Reconnect sway bar end link and brake line bracket; reinstall wheel","Repeat opposite side","Lift rear of vehicle; remove rear wheels","Support rear axle under differential","Disconnect rear sway bar end links","Remove and replace rear shocks (two bolts each \u2014 upper and lower mount)","Reinstall wheels, lower vehicle","Bounce suspension to settle; torque all fasteners at ride height","Verify ride height front and rear; adjust front collar if needed for level stance","Get an alignment \u2014 even at 2\" the alignment shifts enough to affect tire wear"],"pros":["Clean direct-replacement install with no geometry changes required at 2\" lift","Bilstein 5100 is a proven monotube design \u2014 significantly better than OEM damping for trail and washboard","Front height adjustment collar allows fine-tuning for precise level stance","No KDSS system involvement on non-KDSS models \u2014 straightforward swap","Fits 285/75R16 (33\") tires with minor fender trimming at 2\" lift"],"cons":["Non-KDSS only \u2014 standard shocks will compress the KDSS system incorrectly on KDSS-equipped models","Does not address CV angle or caster above 2\" \u2014 UCAs required for 2.5\"+","Rough Country kit uses lower-quality shocks \u2014 acceptable for mild use, not recommended for regular trail driving","Spacer lifts (Icon Stage 1) retain factory damper \u2014 if factory shocks are worn, you're just adding lift without improving damping","Get an alignment \u2014 it's not optional after a lift, even at this height"]},{"id":"4runner-coilover-replacement","title":"Coilover Replacement \u2014 Bilstein 6112 / Icon / Old Man Emu","vehicle":"4runner","category":"suspension","subcategory":"coilovers","type":"part","difficulty":3,"time_hours":{"low":3,"high":6},"cost_usd":{"low":700,"high":2200},"tools_required":["floor jack","jack stands","metric sockets (12, 14, 17, 19, 22mm)","torque wrench","pry bar","tie rod separator","alignment-pen marker"],"skills_required":["suspension disassembly","torque sequencing","alignment understanding"],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","mild_trail","moderate_trail","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2010-2024"],"tags":["lift","coilovers","front-suspension","ride-quality"],"parts":[{"name":"Bilstein 6112 Front Coilover Kit (5th Gen 4Runner)","part_number":"47-310025","vendor":"Bilstein","price_estimate_usd":850},{"name":"Bilstein 5160 Rear Reservoir Shocks (5th Gen 4Runner)","vendor":"Bilstein","price_estimate_usd":425},{"name":"Icon Stage 2 4Runner Suspension Kit","vendor":"Icon Vehicle Dynamics","price_estimate_usd":1850},{"name":"Old Man Emu BP-51 Front Coilover Set","vendor":"ARB / OME","price_estimate_usd":1650},{"name":"Dobinsons IMS Front Strut (assembled)","vendor":"Dobinsons","price_estimate_usd":720}],"citations":[{"title":"Bilstein 6112 Install on 5th Gen 4Runner (Trail4Runner)","url":"https://trail4runner.com/2018/07/03/bilstein-6112-5100-install-5th-gen-4runner/"},{"title":"Full List of Suspension Torque Specs For the 5th Gen 4Runner","url":"https://trail4runner.com/2020/06/17/5th-gen-4runner-torque-specs/"},{"title":"Bilstein 47-310025 Front 6112 Kit (Exit Offroad)","url":"https://exitoffroad.com/product/bilstein-47-310025-front-6112-series-kit/"}],"alternatives":["4runner-upper-control-arms-4runner","4runner-lower-ball-joint-4runner"],"summary":"Bilstein 6112 coilovers give the 5th gen 4Runner a 1.5\"\u20133.2\" front lift with better damping than stock and bolt-in installation \u2014 pair with 5160 rear shocks for the most common build path.","description":"The 5th gen 4Runner (2010-2024) ships with soft front coilover struts tuned for highway ride. They sag noticeably under bumper, winch, and skid plate weight, and they fade hard on washboard. The two most common upgrade paths are Bilstein 6112 (best value, ~$850 front) and Icon Stage 2 (better damping, ~$1850 for the matched kit). Old Man Emu BP-51 and Dobinsons IMS sit in between.\n\nThe 6112 is a 60mm-piston monotube with a snap-ring-adjustable spring perch \u2014 five positions giving 1.5\", 1.85\", 2.25\", 2.75\", and 3.2\" of front lift. Most builders run the middle position (around 2.25\") which gives clearance for 33\" tires without burning through CV axles. Going to 3.2\" puts the front CV angles past their happy range \u2014 expect shorter CV life unless you also drop the differential.\n\nPair with Bilstein 5160 remote-reservoir shocks at the rear (about $425/pair) for a matched-tune ride. 5160s help with brake fade and heat dissipation on long descents and have rebuildable internals. If you're going further than 2\" of rear lift, you need new rear coils too (not just longer shocks) \u2014 Bilstein doesn't make them, so source from Icon or OME.\n\nInstall requires breaking the upper ball joint (or removing the upper control arm), separating the tie rod end, and dropping the lower strut bolt. The struts come pre-assembled \u2014 no spring compressor needed. Key torque values (verify against the trail4runner torque list before final tightening): upper strut nuts 47 ft-lb; lower strut bolt 100 ft-lb; tie rod castle nut 67 ft-lb; upper ball joint nut 80 ft-lb. Alignment is mandatory after \u2014 caster and toe both change.\n\nIf you're doing the upper control arms at the same time (recommended for any lift above 2\"), order those first so they're ready to install alongside.","steps":["Park on level ground; loosen front lug nuts.","Lift the front; support the frame on jack stands; remove front wheels.","Disconnect the sway bar end links from the lower control arms.","Separate the tie rod end from the steering knuckle (penetrant, tie rod separator).","Remove the upper ball joint nut; pop the upper control arm free from the knuckle (or remove the UCA entirely).","Support the lower control arm with a floor jack.","Remove the three upper strut nuts from the strut tower.","Remove the lower strut bolt at the lower control arm.","Drop the old strut down and out.","Install the new 6112 with the spring perch at your chosen lift height (factory-set or your choice).","Snug the upper nuts; install the lower bolt with the suspension at ride height (load the LCA with the floor jack before final torque).","Reconnect tie rod (67 ft-lb), upper ball joint (80 ft-lb), sway bar end links.","Repeat the other side.","Torque upper strut nuts to 47 ft-lb; lower strut bolt to 100 ft-lb (at ride height).","Reinstall wheels; torque lug nuts to 83 ft-lb.","Drive immediately to alignment shop \u2014 do not skip this step."],"pros":["Bilstein 6112 is the best damper-per-dollar in the 4Runner aftermarket","Spring perch adjustability lets you tune lift height after install","Pre-assembled \u2014 no spring compressor","Rear 5160 reservoirs add real cooling capacity for trail use"],"cons":["Lift above 2.25\" stresses CV axles; budget for shorter CV life","Alignment required immediately after","Upper control arms become a near-mandatory companion upgrade above 2\" lift","6112 is fixed-valving \u2014 not adjustable like Icon or OME BP-51"]},{"id":"4runner-expedition-lift","title":"Expedition Lift \u2014 Long-Travel and Dual Remote Reservoir ($2,500+)","vehicle":"4runner","category":"suspension","type":"kit","difficulty":5,"time_hours":{"low":20,"high":32},"cost_usd":{"low":2500,"high":6000},"tools_required":["floor jack","jack stands","torque wrench","socket set","spring compressor","ball joint press","welder or fab shop access","angle grinder","breaker bar","pry bar","drill and bits"],"skills_required":["advanced suspension work","fabrication basics","shock tuning","alignment"],"build_tiers":["moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2010-2024"],"tags":["lift","suspension","long-travel","remote-reservoir","expedition","KDSS-delete"],"parts":[{"name":"Icon Vehicle Dynamics Stage 3 Coilovers + Rear Shocks (5th Gen 4Runner)","vendor":"Icon Vehicle Dynamics","price_estimate_usd":2499},{"name":"Fox 2.5 Performance Elite Remote Reservoir Coilovers (5th Gen 4Runner)","vendor":"Fox Racing Shox","price_estimate_usd":1999},{"name":"Total Chaos Fabrication Long-Travel Suspension Kit (5th Gen 4Runner)","vendor":"Total Chaos Fabrication","price_estimate_usd":4200},{"name":"King Shocks OEM Performance+ Remote Reservoir (5th Gen 4Runner)","vendor":"King Shocks","price_estimate_usd":2799}],"citations":[{"title":"Icon Vehicle Dynamics Stage 3 4Runner Product Page","url":"https://www.iconvehicledynamics.com"},{"title":"Total Chaos 4Runner Long-Travel Kit","url":"https://www.totalchaos.com"}],"alternatives":["4runner-mid-lift","4runner-budget-lift"],"summary":"The expedition lift tier for the 4Runner means 3\"\u20134\" of lift with remote reservoir coilovers, extended UCAs, and \u2014 for full long-travel \u2014 a KDSS delete that trades the factory hydraulic sway bar for fixed end links and dramatically more suspension travel.","description":"At the expedition tier, the 4Runner build follows two distinct paths. The first is a high-end coilover upgrade with extended UCAs \u2014 Icon Stage 3, Fox 2.5 PE, or King OEM Performance+ \u2014 which keeps the stock geometry footprint but adds 12\"+ of high-speed damping performance. This path is KDSS-compatible on KDSS-equipped trucks (specify at order), and it's the correct approach for overland rigs that need refined high-speed comfort and a clean build without irreversible modifications.\n\nThe second path is Total Chaos long-travel, which replaces the entire front suspension geometry with extended lower control arms, a wider track, and repositioned shock mounts. This delivers 12\"\u201314\" of wheel travel and transforms the 4Runner into a genuine high-speed trail machine. Full long-travel kits on KDSS-equipped 4Runners require a KDSS delete \u2014 you replace the KDSS hydraulic end links with fixed end links and remove the KDSS system. This is irreversible in the sense that you'd need to source replacement KDSS parts and rebuild to return to stock. The payoff is unrestricted suspension travel and the ability to run larger shocks that the KDSS plumbing physically prevented.\n\nKDSS delete is not a decision to make lightly if you use the 4Runner daily. On pavement, KDSS meaningfully reduces body roll and improves stability. Without it, you'll notice more roll on on-ramp entries and spirited driving. On the trail, you won't miss it. If the 4Runner is a dedicated trail and overland rig that sees normal roads mostly as transit between trailheads, KDSS delete is fine. If it's a genuine daily driver that also goes off-road, preserve the KDSS and spec the KDSS-compatible high-end shock route instead.\n\nRemote reservoir coilovers (Fox 2.5, Icon 2.5 VS, King OEM+) hold more fluid and run cooler on repeated high-speed hits. The reservoir doubles the oil volume, which significantly delays thermal fade on desert washboards or repeated technical obstacles. At this price tier, the damping quality is the primary deliverable \u2014 these shocks are tunable in the field (compression adjustment knob) and rebuildable for long-term ownership.","steps":["Decide: KDSS-compatible high-end coilovers, or full long-travel with KDSS delete \u2014 plan accordingly","Order complete kit with matching UCAs and rear shocks; do not mix front-tier with budget rear","If KDSS delete: obtain fixed sway bar end links and bracket set before starting; remove KDSS hydraulic lines and cylinders first and cap all ports per manufacturer guidance","Lift and fully support vehicle on jack stands","Remove front suspension assembly completely: shocks, UCAs, lower control arms (on long-travel), sway bar","Install new lower control arm brackets and arms per kit template for long-travel; or install new extended UCAs only for coilover-only upgrade","Install new coilovers; set preload per shock manufacturer spec for target ride height","Install bumpstop extensions per kit requirements","Reconnect sway bar (with fixed end links if KDSS delete) or install long-travel sway bar system","Reinstall brakes and wheels","Complete rear: install new shocks; replace or rebuild rear suspension per kit instructions","Lower vehicle; bounce to settle","Drive to alignment shop \u2014 extended-travel geometry requires specialist alignment","Break in shocks per manufacturer protocol; recheck and adjust compression valve after 500 miles"],"pros":["Remote reservoir coilovers at this tier are rebuildable and tunable \u2014 long-term ownership investment","Icon Stage 3 KDSS-compatible option allows full expedition performance without irreversible modifications","Long-travel kits deliver 12\"\u201314\" of wheel travel \u2014 the highest-performance IFS 4Runner front end available","Fox 2.5 PE shocks handle desert high-speed terrain with consistent damping on repeated hits","King shocks are field-rebuildable in remote locations with basic tools"],"cons":["KDSS delete is effectively irreversible without sourcing and reinstalling OEM KDSS components","Long-travel kits require inner fender modification or cutting in some configurations","Total cost for full long-travel with rear conversion approaches $8,000\u2013$10,000 complete","Extended-travel builds require an alignment specialist \u2014 standard shops may not have 4Runner long-travel specs","Expedition tier is genuinely unnecessary for most trail and overland use \u2014 mid-tier lift delivers 90% of the capability at 40% of the cost"]},{"id":"4runner-kdss-bleed-4runner","title":"KDSS System Bleed and Leak Inspection","vehicle":"4runner","category":"suspension","type":"task","difficulty":4,"time_hours":{"low":2,"high":4},"cost_usd":{"low":60,"high":250},"tools_required":["floor jack","jack stands","10mm and 12mm wrenches","torque wrench","fluid catch pan","flashlight","KDSS bleeder pump or hand pressure pump (1015 psi)"],"skills_required":["intermediate wrenching","hydraulic systems comfort"],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","mild_trail","moderate_trail","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2010-2024"],"tags":["suspension","KDSS","fluid","TRD Off-Road","Trail"],"parts":[{"name":"Toyota KDSS hydraulic fluid (1L)","part_number":"08886-80506","vendor":"Toyota","price_estimate_usd":38},{"name":"KDSS bleeder valve seal kit","vendor":"Toyota dealer","price_estimate_usd":25},{"name":"Replacement accumulator (only if pressure fault)","part_number":"48830-60130","vendor":"Toyota","price_estimate_usd":850}],"citations":[{"title":"Toyota 4Runner KDSS Bleeding Service Manual","url":"https://www.to4runner.net/bleeding-1189.html"},{"title":"Lexus GX KDSS Bleed Procedure (PDF)","url":"https://lexusgxor.com/_media/general:kdss_bleed.pdf"},{"title":"Carspec MN \u2014 KDSS service and repair","url":"https://carspecmn.com/toyota-and-lexus-kdss-service-and-repair-gx460-gx460-4runner-and-land-cruiser/"}],"alternatives":["4runner-coilover-replacement-4runner","4runner-upper-control-arms-4runner"],"summary":"KDSS is Toyota's hydraulic sway bar system on Trail and TRD Off-Road 4Runners; it rarely fails, but if you've lifted the truck or notice uneven ride height side-to-side, it needs a bleed to redistribute pressure and check for leaks.","description":"KDSS (Kinetic Dynamic Suspension System) connects the front and rear sway bars with hydraulic cylinders and an accumulator. On-road it locks up for flat cornering; off-road it goes slack to allow articulation. The system uses high-pressure hydraulic fluid running through hard lines and bleeders at the cylinders. It's sealed from the factory and Toyota lists no service interval \u2014 which means most owners forget it exists until something looks wrong.\n\nThe two most common reasons to touch the KDSS system: you've installed a lift and the front-to-rear ride height looks off, or you spot fluid on the underbody near the front cylinder or accumulator. If both bleeders are at different heights side-to-side after a lift, the cylinders are at different positions and need to be re-equalized. Skipping this leaves the system preloaded \u2014 one side carries more spring force than the other, and the truck will sag or lean.\n\nThe bleed procedure requires applying 7 MPa (1015 psi) to the system through the bleeder valves while cycling them in sequence. You can do this with a Toyota SST high-pressure pump or build a DIY setup from a $40 hand pump and a hydraulic gauge \u2014 many owners do, and it works. Do not exceed 8 MPa (1160 psi) or you risk damaging the accumulator. Bleeding without pressure does nothing \u2014 you have to push fluid, not just open valves.\n\nLeak inspection is mostly visual: check the front cylinder seal (behind the front sway bar, driver side), the rear cylinder, the lines running along the frame, and the accumulator (mid-frame, passenger side). Any wet hydraulic film means a seal is going. Replace before fluid level drops enough to introduce air.\n\nA complete bleed takes about 6 liters of fresh fluid. Use Toyota part 08886-80506 specifically \u2014 generic hydraulic oils have wrong viscosity and will damage seals.\n\nThe shop labor on this is $400-$600 because most independents won't touch it. If you have the tools and patience, doing it yourself is well within reach.","steps":["Park on level ground. Check ride height at all four corners and note the differences.","Locate both bleeder valves: one on the front stabilizer control cylinder (driver side, just rearward of the front diff), one on the stabilizer control with accumulator housing (mid-frame, passenger side).","Wipe both bleeders clean. Inspect surrounding lines and cylinders for any sign of leak.","Connect your pressure pump to the front cylinder bleeder. Apply 7 MPa (1015 psi). Stay below 8 MPa.","Loosen the front bleeder slightly to let air escape with fluid. Close. Repeat until output is clean fluid with no bubbles.","Move to the rear cylinder bleeder. Apply 7 MPa, loosen, close, repeat.","Move to the accumulator housing bleeder. Same procedure.","Cycle steps 4-7 until all three bleed points are running clean fluid.","Final torque: bleeder plugs to 11 ft-lb. Wipe fluid off, check for new leaks.","Drive 5-10 miles. Re-check ride height and re-bleed if side-to-side variance remains."],"pros":["Restores even ride height and articulation","Catches leaks before the system fails","DIY-able with a hand pressure pump and Toyota fluid","No alignment needed after"],"cons":["Wrong fluid will damage seals","Exceeding 8 MPa damages the accumulator","Most independent shops decline this work","Doing it without pressure (just opening valves) accomplishes nothing"]},{"id":"4runner-lower-ball-joint-4runner","title":"Lower Ball Joint Replacement \u2014 4th and 5th Gen","vehicle":"4runner","category":"suspension","subcategory":"ball_joints","type":"task","difficulty":4,"time_hours":{"low":4,"high":8},"cost_usd":{"low":180,"high":480},"tools_required":["ball joint press kit","torque wrench (250+ ft-lb capable)","30mm and 32mm sockets","pickle fork or ball joint separator","snap ring pliers","heavy hammer","penetrating oil"],"skills_required":["heavy suspension work","press tool use","safety discipline"],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2003-2009","2010-2024"],"tags":["ball-joint","suspension","safety-critical","lca"],"parts":[{"name":"Toyota OEM lower ball joint (pair)","part_number":"43330-39755","vendor":"Toyota","price_estimate_usd":180},{"name":"555 (Japanese OEM supplier) ball joint","part_number":"SB-T872","vendor":"555 Sankei","price_estimate_usd":90},{"name":"Moog K500080 (limited-life aftermarket)","part_number":"K500080","vendor":"Moog","price_estimate_usd":60}],"citations":[{"title":"Toyota Service Information System \u2014 1GR lower ball joint torque specs","url":"https://techinfo.toyota.com"},{"title":"4Runner forum: ball joint failure thread and OEM vs aftermarket data","url":"https://www.toyota-4runner.org"},{"title":"NHTSA: 1996-2002 4Runner ball joint recall history (precursor to current design)","url":"https://www.nhtsa.gov"}],"alternatives":["4runner-upper-control-arms-4runner"],"summary":"Toyota OEM lower ball joints (43330-39755) are the only ones that reliably last 100K+ miles on a 4Runner. Aftermarket budget joints fail in 20\u201340K. The joint presses into the lower control arm; total job is 4\u20138 hours per side and demands a ball joint press kit. Castle nut torques to 105 ft-lb.","description":"The lower ball joint connects the lower control arm to the steering knuckle on every IFS 4Runner (1996\u2013present). When it fails, the wheel tucks under the truck \u2014 at speed, this is a wreck. There is no safe \"wait and see\" with a clunking ball joint.\n\n**OEM-only is not optional.** The 1996\u20132002 4Runner had a famous ball joint recall (NHTSA Campaign 04V-432) because the design was undersized. Toyota redesigned it for the 4th gen (2003+) and the OEM joint has been reliable since. Aftermarket Moog, Beck/Arnley, and parts-store-brand joints use thinner stamped housings and inferior grease seals; they fail at 20K\u201340K miles. This is the one part on a 4Runner where you do not save money \u2014 Toyota OEM only (43330-39755 for lower; 555 SB-T872 is the only acceptable Japanese OEM-equivalent alternative).\n\n**Symptom checklist.** Clunking over speed bumps at low speed. Steering wander on the highway that's not corrected by alignment. Uneven inside-edge tire wear that returns within 5K of an alignment. Visible grease at the boot. Any one of these means inspect; two means replace.\n\n**Press tool is required.** The ball joint presses into the lower control arm with significant interference fit. You cannot drive it out with a hammer (you'll bend the arm). Rent or buy a ball joint press kit \u2014 Harbor Freight #66828 is $80 and works.","steps":["Lift front of truck, support frame on stands, remove wheel. Penetrate all suspension bolts you're about to touch with PB Blaster the night before.","Disconnect the sway bar end link from the LCA (14mm).","Remove the lower shock bolt (17mm) \u2014 you'll need to drop the shock to get clearance.","Remove the ABS sensor harness and brake line bracket from the knuckle.","Remove the castle nut on the ball joint stud (32mm). Use a pickle fork or ball joint separator to pop the stud loose from the knuckle.","Remove the LCA from the frame \u2014 two bolts, typically 22mm. Lift the LCA out of the truck.","Remove the snap ring on top of the ball joint with snap ring pliers.","Use the ball joint press kit to press the old joint OUT of the LCA. (Cup on one side, drive sleeve on the other; thread the press tight slowly.)","Clean the bore in the LCA. Apply a thin film of grease to the new joint's outer surface.","Press the new joint IN, going the opposite direction. The joint will seat with the snap ring groove showing on top.","Install the new snap ring.","Reinstall the LCA onto the frame. Torque LCA-to-frame bolts to 137 ft-lb (front) and 100 ft-lb (rear). Tighten the front bolt only at full droop; tighten the rear at ride height.","Insert ball joint stud through the knuckle. Install castle nut, torque to 105 ft-lb, then back off only enough to align the cotter pin slot (never overtighten past alignment). Install cotter pin.","Reconnect shock, sway bar end link, ABS, brake bracket.","Reinstall wheel, lower truck. Re-torque ball joint nut after first 500 miles.","Get an alignment within 50 miles. Camber and caster will be off."],"pros":["Restores steering precision and safety","OEM joint is good for 100K+ miles","Eliminates uneven tire wear from worn joint","Once done, you know the joint history"],"cons":["4\u20138 hours per side","Ball joint press is required","Aftermarket joints fail quickly \u2014 must spend $180 on OEM","Alignment required after","LCA bolts are torqued at ride height, not droop \u2014 easy to miss"]},{"id":"4runner-mid-lift","title":"Mid-Tier Lift with UCAs \u2014 2.5\"\u20133\" Lift ($1,000\u2013$2,500)","vehicle":"4runner","category":"suspension","type":"kit","difficulty":4,"time_hours":{"low":6,"high":10},"cost_usd":{"low":1000,"high":2500},"tools_required":["floor jack","jack stands","torque wrench","socket set","ball joint press","spring compressor","breaker bar","pry bar"],"skills_required":["suspension work","intermediate wrenching","ball joint service","torque specs"],"build_tiers":["mild_trail","moderate_trail","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2010-2024"],"tags":["lift","suspension","UCA","KDSS","coilover","mid-lift"],"parts":[{"name":"Icon Vehicle Dynamics Stage 2 Complete Lift Kit (5th Gen 4Runner)","vendor":"Icon Vehicle Dynamics","price_estimate_usd":1299},{"name":"Dobinsons MRR Coilovers + UCAs Kit (5th Gen 4Runner)","vendor":"Dobinsons","price_estimate_usd":1399},{"name":"Old Man Emu BP-51 Bypass Shock Kit (5th Gen 4Runner)","vendor":"Old Man Emu","price_estimate_usd":1499},{"name":"Icon Vehicle Dynamics Billet Upper Control Arms (5th Gen 4Runner)","vendor":"Icon Vehicle Dynamics","price_estimate_usd":749}],"citations":[{"title":"Icon Vehicle Dynamics 4Runner Stage 2 Product Page","url":"https://www.iconvehicledynamics.com"},{"title":"Dobinsons 4Runner Suspension Products","url":"https://www.dobinsons.com"}],"alternatives":["4runner-budget-lift","4runner-expedition-lift"],"summary":"The mid-tier lift on the 4Runner is where KDSS compatibility becomes the deciding factor \u2014 Icon Stage 2, Dobinsons MRR, and OME BP-51 all have KDSS-compatible shock valving options, but you must order the correct variant for your trim.","description":"At 2.5\"\u20133\" of lift, the 4Runner's IFS geometry requires the same fix as the Tacoma: upper control arms to correct caster, restore CV shaft angle, and provide more droop travel. The OEM UCAs on the 5th gen 4Runner are aluminum-bodied units that are not adjustable \u2014 at 2.5\" they push the ball joint to an angle that causes premature wear. Aftermarket UCAs restore the correct geometry for lifted height.\n\nKDSS compatibility is the critical specification for this tier. KDSS-equipped 4Runners (Trail, TRD Pro, some Limited) use hydraulic sway bar cylinders that require specific shock damping rates to function correctly. Installing a standard shock on a KDSS-equipped 4Runner compresses the KDSS circuit improperly, causing the sway bar to bind or the hydraulic lines to be over-pressurized on articulation. Icon's CDCV (Continuous Damping Control Valve) coilovers and Fox's remote reservoir units are specifically valved for KDSS 4Runners. Check the product description for \"KDSS compatible\" before ordering any shock or coilover kit.\n\nFor non-KDSS 4Runners, the mid-tier lift is more straightforward \u2014 any of the listed kits work cleanly. Icon Stage 2 is the most complete package (UCAs, coilovers, rear shocks, add-a-leaf). Dobinsons MRR offers remote reservoir coilovers at a competitive price with excellent tunability. OME BP-51 is the premium choice with internal bypass damping that handles rough terrain exceptionally well at this price point.\n\nUCAs for the 4Runner from Total Chaos ($949), Camburg ($899), or Icon ($749) all fit correctly and are meaningfully better than OEM. For a trail-focused build, Total Chaos billet UCAs are the most durable; Icon UCAs are the best value.","steps":["Confirm KDSS vs non-KDSS configuration; order KDSS-compatible shocks if applicable","Lift and support front of vehicle; remove front wheels","Disconnect OEM front strut assembly (sway bar end link, brake line bracket, strut-to-knuckle bolts, upper mount)","Disconnect OEM upper control arm from frame and spindle \u2014 use ball joint separator on spindle connection","Install new UCAs: torque frame bolts snug (final torque at ride height); connect new ball joint to spindle and torque","Install new coilover; set height collar per manufacturer spec for target lift","Reconnect sway bar end link and brake line; reinstall wheel","Repeat opposite side","Lift and support rear; remove rear wheels; support axle","Disconnect rear sway bar end links","Replace rear shocks; install add-a-leaf or full rear spring per kit instructions","Reassemble rear; torque at ride height","Lower vehicle; bounce to settle; perform final torque on all suspension fasteners at ride height","Drive to alignment shop \u2014 request 4Runner-specific caster and camber specs for lifted height"],"pros":["KDSS-compatible shock options allow full mid-lift on Trail and TRD Pro trims without disabling KDSS","UCAs correct the geometry that makes everything else work better \u2014 steering feel, tire wear, CV longevity","Icon Stage 2 is the most complete all-in-one kit for this tier \u2014 no compatibility guesswork","OME BP-51 delivers bypass-style damping at a price point below most US boutique shocks","285/75R16 (33\") tires fit cleanly on most 4Runners at 2.5\"\u20133\" lift without trimming"],"cons":["KDSS compatibility error is expensive to fix \u2014 double-check spec before ordering","Ball joint press required for UCA swap \u2014 not an optional tool","Requires professional alignment after install \u2014 budget $100\u2013$150","Dobinsons MRR valving is firm; less comfortable on pavement than Icon or OME for daily drivers","3\" lift on a KDSS 4Runner reduces KDSS effectiveness somewhat \u2014 the system works best at stock-adjacent heights"]},{"id":"4runner-rear-lift-options","title":"Rear Lift Options \u2014 Spacers vs. New Coils","vehicle":"4runner","category":"suspension","type":"part","difficulty":2,"time_hours":{"low":2,"high":5},"cost_usd":{"low":80,"high":700},"tools_required":["floor jack","jack stands","metric sockets 17mm-22mm","torque wrench","breaker bar"],"skills_required":["suspension R&R","coil compression awareness"],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","mild_trail","moderate_trail","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2010-2024"],"tags":["lift","leveling","ride-quality","overland"],"parts":[{"name":"Daystar 1.5\" rear coil spacer kit","part_number":"KT09125BK","vendor":"Daystar","price_estimate_usd":90},{"name":"Old Man Emu 2890 rear coils (medium load)","part_number":"2890","vendor":"ARB / OME","price_estimate_usd":320},{"name":"Dobinsons C59-319V variable rate rear coils","part_number":"C59-319V","vendor":"Dobinsons","price_estimate_usd":360},{"name":"Eibach Pro-Truck stage 2 rear coils","vendor":"Eibach","price_estimate_usd":280},{"name":"Icon Stage 2 rear coil and shock kit","vendor":"Icon Vehicle Dynamics","price_estimate_usd":700}],"citations":[{"title":"5th Gen 4Runner Lift & Leveling Kits: Beginners Guide","url":"https://trail4runner.com/2017/06/14/5th-gen-4runner-mods-lift-kits/comment-page-1/"},{"title":"Top 10 5th Gen 4Runner Suspension Lift Kits","url":"https://trail4runner.com/2020/12/18/top-10-5th-gen-4runner-suspension-lift-kits/"},{"title":"Eibach vs Dobinson vs OME (4Runners.com)","url":"https://www.4runners.com/threads/eibach-vs-dobinson-vs-ome.6417/"}],"alternatives":[],"summary":"If you only drive on pavement and want the truck level, a $100 rear spacer is fine. If you carry a roof rack, drawer system, or bumper, swap to new rear coils \u2014 spacers preload the stock spring, accelerate sag, and ride worse on every surface.","description":"The 5th-gen 4Runner sags in the rear from the day you drive it home, and any added weight makes it worse. Stock rear coils were specced for a near-empty cargo area; bolt on a roof tent, sliders, a rear bumper with a tire carrier, and a Trasharoo full of recovery gear and you'll measure two-plus inches of rear droop within a few months. There are two ways to get the rear back up: a spacer that sits on top of the stock coil, or replacement coils sized for your real load.\n\nSpacers are cheaper. A Daystar 1.5-inch rear coil spacer kit runs about $90 and installs in two hours. The catch is that a spacer doesn't fix anything \u2014 it pre-compresses the stock spring, which means the truck rides on a stiffer portion of the spring curve and the spring is closer to coil bind under articulation. Forum consensus on 4Runners.com and Trail4Runner is consistent: spacers ride harshly compared to new coils, both on and off-road. They're acceptable on a stock-weight, mall-crawler 4Runner that needs to look level. They're a bad answer on a built truck.\n\nNew coils are the right answer for any 4Runner carrying real weight. The three brands that come up over and over: Old Man Emu (OME 2890 medium-load coils at about $320 give 2 to 2.5 inches with up to 250 lb constant load), Dobinsons (variable-rate C59 series, around $360, broad load ratings), and Eibach Pro-Truck (around $280, slightly firmer ride). Pair them with appropriate shocks \u2014 Bilstein 5100s at $80 each for a budget setup, Bilstein 5160 remote reservoirs for heat dissipation on long trails, or Icon 2.0/2.5 series for a high-end build. Spring rate matters more than brand: get a constant-load coil rated for the weight you actually carry. OME's \"medium\" is right for a roof rack and some recovery gear. \"Heavy\" is right for a rear bumper and tire carrier. Going too heavy ride harshly; too light squats under load.\n\nA common mistake: installing rear lift coils without addressing the upper control arms or sub-frame contact points. Once the rear is up 2+ inches, the rear shock may run out of droop travel \u2014 check shock length and consider extended-travel rear shocks if you've gone above 2.5 inches.","steps":["Weigh the truck loaded with everything you usually carry (CAT scale or estimate by adding component weights).","Pick a coil rated for that real-world weight \u2014 not stock weight.","Remove the rear wheels; support the axle with a floor jack.","Disconnect the rear sway bar end links and the rear shock lower bolts to let the axle drop further.","Disconnect the rear track bar at the axle (leave the frame side).","Lower the axle until the stock coils are loose; pull them out by hand.","Install new coils with the isolator pads. Use a coil retainer if articulation is unrestricted.","Reconnect shocks, end links, and track bar. Torque to spec.","Drive 10 miles and re-measure ride height. Allow 100 miles for coils to settle."],"pros":["New coils restore actual spring capacity, not just height","Better ride on every surface compared to spacers","Wide load-rating selection from OME, Dobinsons, Eibach","Two-hour install with hand tools"],"cons":["Spacers preload the stock spring and ride harshly under articulation","May need extended rear shocks if going above 2.5\"","Heavy-load coils ride stiff when the truck is empty","Coil spring R&R can pinch fingers \u2014 control the axle drop carefully"]},{"id":"4runner-upper-control-arms","title":"Upper Control Arm Upgrade After Lift \u2014 5th Gen 4Runner","vehicle":"4runner","category":"suspension","type":"part","difficulty":3,"time_hours":{"low":2,"high":4},"cost_usd":{"low":550,"high":1100},"tools_required":["floor jack","jack stands","metric sockets","torque wrench","pickle fork or tie rod separator","pry bar"],"skills_required":["intermediate wrenching","ball joint work","alignment understanding"],"build_tiers":["mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2010-2024"],"tags":["lift","caster","uca","ifs"],"parts":[{"name":"SPC Adjustable UCAs 4Runner 5th gen","part_number":"25470","vendor":"SPC","price_estimate_usd":650},{"name":"Total Chaos UCAs 4Runner (uniball)","part_number":"96514","vendor":"Total Chaos","price_estimate_usd":850},{"name":"Icon Delta Joint UCAs 5th gen 4Runner","part_number":"58460DJ","vendor":"Icon","price_estimate_usd":900},{"name":"Dirt King Ball Joint UCAs","vendor":"Dirt King","price_estimate_usd":700}],"citations":[{"title":"Total Chaos UCA Install on 5th Gen 4Runner \u2014 Trail4Runner","url":"https://trail4runner.com/2019/02/14/total-chaos-uca-install-toyota-4runner/"},{"title":"6 Upper Control Arm Setups For 5th Gen 4Runner \u2014 Trail4Runner","url":"https://trail4runner.com/2023/07/07/upper-control-arm-setups-5th-gen-4runner/"},{"title":"SPC 4Runner Adjustable UCAs \u2014 4Runner Customs","url":"https://4rcustoms.com/spc-4th-5th-gen-4runner-adjustable-upper-control-arms/"}],"alternatives":["4runner-coilover-replacement","4runner-rear-lift-options","4runner-lower-ball-joint"],"summary":"If you've lifted a 5th gen 4Runner more than 2 inches and the alignment shop tells you caster is at the lower spec, you need adjustable upper control arms \u2014 the stock arms run out of caster adjustment before you do.","description":"The 5th gen 4Runner is a double-wishbone IFS truck. When you raise the front by extending the coilover, the upper control arm pivots downward at the spindle end and the caster angle goes negative. The alignment cam adjustment at the lower control arm can recover a little caster, but at about 2\"\u20132.5\" of lift you'll max out the cams and the alignment shop will hand the truck back with caster at 1\u00b0 or below. The truck will feel vague at highway speed, wander when crowned roads transition, and won't self-center coming out of a turn.\n\nAftermarket adjustable UCAs solve this by either re-locating the ball joint higher in the arm body (Dirt King) or providing a uniball at the spindle end that lets the arm swing through a wider range (Total Chaos, Camburg). Either way, you recover 2\u00b0\u20133\u00b0 of caster and the truck drives like Toyota intended again. SPC's arms are the budget pick at $600\u2013$700, use a sealed ball joint, and are quiet. Total Chaos and Icon use uniballs that flex more on articulation but make noise as they wear and need rebuilding every 30K\u201350K trail miles. Dirt King's ball joint version splits the difference \u2014 quiet like SPC, more articulation than stock.\n\nThe choice between sealed ball joint and uniball isn't really about caster \u2014 both give you caster back. It's about how the truck will be used. A 4Runner that's daily-driven 25K miles a year with occasional trail trips wants SPC or Dirt King. A 4Runner that lives off-road wants Total Chaos or Icon, with the understanding that you're committing to a uniball rebuild program. Anyone telling you uniballs \"don't wear\" is selling them.\n\nInstallation is straightforward IFS work. The upper ball joint comes apart with a pickle fork or tie rod separator. The two pivot bolts at the frame are 19mm and torqued in the 80\u201390 ft-lb range. Brake hose bracket relocates to the new arm. ABS sensor wire either re-routes or gets a new bracket from the UCA kit. None of the kits require modifying the frame \u2014 these are bolt-in. After install, the alignment shop will dial in caster between +2\u00b0 and +4\u00b0 depending on whether you want quick steering response (low) or stable highway tracking (high).\n\nThe KDSS-equipped 4Runner (Trail Premium, TRD Off-Road Premium, TRD Pro) doesn't change the UCA install \u2014 KDSS is a sway bar system, not a control arm system. You don't need to bleed KDSS to install UCAs.","steps":["Park on level ground, chock rear wheels.","Loosen front lug nuts (22mm). Lift the front, support on stands under the frame, let the lower control arm hang.","Remove front wheel.","Support the lower control arm with a floor jack \u2014 keeps the coilover loaded.","Remove the ABS sensor wire and brake hose bracket from the stock UCA.","Break the upper ball joint cotter pin, remove the nut (19mm).","Separate the ball joint from the spindle with a pickle fork or ball joint press.","Remove the two UCA pivot bolts at the frame (19mm).","Pull the stock UCA out.","Install the new UCA. Pivot bolts go to factory torque (80\u201390 ft-lb).","Slot the new ball joint into the spindle, torque the nut (60\u201375 ft-lb depending on brand), install cotter pin.","Re-mount the brake hose bracket and ABS wire on the new arm.","Reinstall the wheel, lug nuts to 83 ft-lb.","Lower the truck.","Get a four-wheel alignment. Caster target: +2.5\u00b0 to +3.5\u00b0. Camber: 0\u00b0 to +0.5\u00b0. Toe: factory spec."],"pros":["Recovers caster after 2\"+ lift","Lets the alignment shop actually dial in spec","SPC and Dirt King options are sealed ball joint \u2014 no rebuild program","Bolt-in install, no frame modification","Stops the wandering and vague highway feel after a lift"],"cons":["Uniball arms need periodic rebuilding (30K\u201350K trail miles)","Quality arms are $600\u2013$900 \u2014 budget for them when planning a lift","Some kits require a brake line relocation or longer line","Alignment after install is required, not optional"]},{"id":"crawl-vs-atrac","title":"CRAWL Control vs. A-TRAC \u2014 When to Use Each","vehicle":"4runner","category":"tools_diagnostic","subcategory":"off_road_systems","type":"diagnostic","difficulty":1,"time_hours":{"low":0,"high":1},"cost_usd":{"low":0,"high":0},"tools_required":[],"skills_required":["basic off-road experience"],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","mild_trail","moderate_trail","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2010-2024"],"tags":["crawl-control","a-trac","trd-off-road","trd-pro","4lo"],"parts":[],"citations":[{"title":"5th Gen 4Runner Off-Road Features \u2014 Trail4Runner","url":"https://trail4runner.com/2018/01/30/5th-gen-4runner-off-road-features/"},{"title":"What is the difference between ATRAC and Crawl control? \u2014 Toyota 4Runner Forum","url":"https://www.toyota-4runner.org/5th-gen-t4rs/156373-what-difference-between-atrac-crawl-control.html"}],"alternatives":["multi-terrain-select-explained"],"summary":"Use A-TRAC when you need brake-based traction at any low speed and want to stay on the throttle yourself; use CRAWL Control when terrain is steep or technical enough that you'd rather hand off throttle and brakes to the truck and focus only on steering.","description":"Both systems live on 5th-gen 4Runner TRD Off-Road, TRD Pro, and Trail trims, and both require 4LO to engage. That's where the similarity ends. Mixing them up \u2014 or not knowing which one is doing the work \u2014 leaves drivers either fighting the truck or missing the easier line through an obstacle.\n\nA-TRAC (Active Traction Control) is the system that pulses the brakes on a spinning wheel to send torque across the differential to the wheel with grip. It's effectively a brake-based locker simulation. You're still driving \u2014 modulating throttle, steering, and brakes yourself \u2014 and A-TRAC works in the background to keep you moving when one or two tires lose contact. It's the right tool for moderate obstacles where you have one wheel in the air on a flex line, slick rock with intermittent grip, or mud where momentum matters and you need to feed throttle.\n\nCRAWL Control is more aggressive automation. You select a speed (1\u20135 mph), lift off the throttle and brake entirely, and steer. The truck handles everything else, pulsing throttle and brakes individually to each wheel to maintain that exact speed. It's loud \u2014 you'll hear the ABS pump groaning and the engine surging \u2014 but it's incredibly effective on terrain where smooth, slow, consistent input matters more than driver feel. Steep rocky descents, deep sand at controlled speed, and difficult ledges where modulating four pedals with two feet is genuinely hard are where CRAWL earns its keep.\n\nToyota's own guidance is to use speeds 1\u20132 for descents, 3\u20135 for ascents, and any speed on flat technical terrain. Lower settings give the system more time to react. Setting 5 in deep sand keeps you from bogging down.\n\nWhere drivers go wrong: leaving CRAWL Control engaged in terrain that doesn't need it. The system constantly cycles the ABS, which wears the pump and is unnecessary on moderate dirt roads where A-TRAC alone (or no electronics at all) would handle the conditions. Treat CRAWL Control like a tool you reach for in genuinely difficult spots, not a default setting.\n\nMulti-Terrain Select (MTS) is a separate adjustment that modifies throttle response and traction sensitivity for surface type. It works alongside both A-TRAC and CRAWL \u2014 it doesn't replace either. Mud & Sand allows more wheel spin before A-TRAC intervenes; Rock allows the least.","steps":["Shift transfer case to 4LO with the truck stopped and in neutral.","For A-TRAC: press the A-TRAC button on the overhead console. Drive normally \u2014 system intervenes automatically when wheel slip is detected.","For CRAWL: press the CRAWL button on the overhead console. Use the dial to select speed 1\u20135.","Release throttle and brake. Steer only.","To disengage CRAWL: press the button again, or tap brake/throttle.","To disengage A-TRAC: press button again or shift out of 4LO."],"pros":["A-TRAC: driver retains full control, less wear on brakes","A-TRAC: excellent for momentum-based obstacles","CRAWL: hands-off speed control on technical terrain","CRAWL: superior for steep, slow descents"],"cons":["CRAWL is noisy and wears ABS components with overuse","Neither replaces a real locker \u2014 both are brake-based","Both require 4LO, so highway use is out","New drivers often default to CRAWL when A-TRAC would do the job"]},{"id":"4runner-tire-fitment-33s","title":"Running 33\" Tires on the 5th Gen 4Runner","vehicle":"4runner","category":"wheels_tires","type":"upgrade_path","difficulty":1,"time_hours":{"low":1,"high":2},"cost_usd":{"low":900,"high":1500},"tools_required":["torque wrench","breaker bar or impact","floor jack","jack stands"],"skills_required":["tire mounting","basic wrenching"],"build_tiers":["mild_trail","moderate_trail","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2010-2024"],"tags":["tires","fitment","33s","285","wheels_tires","TPMS"],"parts":[{"name":"BFGoodrich KO2 All-Terrain T/A 285/75R16 (each)","vendor":"BFGoodrich","price_estimate_usd":225},{"name":"Falken Wildpeak AT3W 285/75R16 (each)","vendor":"Falken","price_estimate_usd":215},{"name":"Nitto Terra Grappler G2 285/75R16 (each)","vendor":"Nitto","price_estimate_usd":220},{"name":"Cooper Discoverer ST/MAXX 285/75R16 (each)","vendor":"Cooper","price_estimate_usd":235}],"citations":[{"title":"4Runner World Tire Fitment Guide","url":"https://www.4runner.org"},{"title":"BFGoodrich KO2 Specifications","url":"https://www.bfgoodrichtires.com"}],"alternatives":["4runner-wheels","4runner-mid-lift","4runner-budget-lift"],"summary":"285/75R16 is the cleaner fit for the 5th gen 4Runner at 2\"\u20132.5\" of lift \u2014 it runs without rubbing on most builds, and the 16\" wheel drops the tire into a more affordable and widely available size range than 17\" equivalents.","description":"The 5th gen 4Runner's front fender opening is slightly more generous than the Tacoma's, which means 285/75R16 (33.8\" actual diameter) clears cleanly at 2.5\" of lift without any trimming on most builds. At 2\", these tires may graze the front mud flap bracket at full compression and full lock \u2014 trimming the plastic bracket resolves it in minutes. The 295/70R17 (33.5\" diameter) in the 17\" size is the alternative, but it's more expensive per tire, requires 3\"+ lift for clean clearance on the 4Runner, and the 17\" size reduces sidewall height which is a disadvantage for off-road cushioning and airing down.\n\nTire compound and tread pattern matter more than brand loyalty. The BFG KO2 remains the all-terrain benchmark \u2014 excellent wet traction, proven trail performance, and tread life of 50,000+ miles under normal conditions. The Falken Wildpeak AT3W has overtaken the KO2 in several independent mud and wet traction comparisons at a slightly lower price. Cooper's ST/MAXX is a mud-terrain pattern that's quieter than most MTs while retaining genuine muddy terrain capability \u2014 worth considering if the 4Runner sees mixed use.\n\nTPMS sensors require attention when switching to aftermarket wheels. The 5th gen 4Runner uses a tire pressure monitoring system with sensors clocked to the OEM wheels \u2014 a swap to aftermarket wheels requires either transferring the OEM sensors to the new wheels (possible with most designs) or purchasing aftermarket-compatible sensors. Confirm sensor compatibility with your wheel vendor before ordering. The system will throw a fault code until sensors are reinstalled and relearned.\n\nFor builds running the stock 17x7 OEM wheels, note the relatively conservative 4.5\" backspacing \u2014 285/75R16 in a 16x8 aftermarket wheel with 4.75\"\u20135\" backspacing gives better clearance than cramming a 285 onto the OEM 17\" wheel.","steps":["Confirm current lift height; 285/75R16 requires minimum 2\" for clean fitment, 2.5\" for zero contact","Order tires; plan for simultaneous wheel upgrade if OEM wheels have inadequate backspacing","Have tires mounted and balanced at tire shop; transfer or install TPMS sensors","Install wheels on vehicle; torque lug nuts to 90 ft-lb in star pattern","Test clearance at full lock in both directions and at full bump before trail use","Trim plastic mudflap bracket if needed for 2\" lift clearance","Perform TPMS relearn procedure per owner's manual (or have the shop do it at tire install)","Recheck lug torque at 50 miles"],"pros":["285/75R16 fits cleanly at 2.5\" lift without any trimming on the 5th gen 4Runner","Taller sidewall compared to 295/70R17 \u2014 better trail cushioning and lower air-down pressure threshold","16\" size is more affordable than 17\" equivalents across all tire brands","BFG KO2 and Falken AT3W are proven AT choices in this size with wide dealer availability","No gear ratio change required for normal trail use at 33\" diameter"],"cons":["295/70R17 on the 17\" OEM wheel requires 3\"+ lift \u2014 more clearance needed than most assume","TPMS sensor transfer or replacement adds $50\u2013$150 to the install cost","Taller tires reduce fuel economy 1\u20132 mpg typical \u2014 more pronounced on highway","285/75R16 on OEM 17\" wheels can work but wheel offset may cause inner fender contact on some builds \u2014 confirm before committing","Cooper ST/MAXX is louder on pavement than AT-pattern tires \u2014 a real trade-off for daily drivers"]},{"id":"4runner-wheels","title":"Wheel Selection for the 5th Gen 4Runner","vehicle":"4runner","category":"wheels_tires","type":"part","difficulty":1,"time_hours":{"low":1,"high":2},"cost_usd":{"low":356,"high":1240},"tools_required":["torque wrench","breaker bar or impact","floor jack","jack stands"],"skills_required":["tire mounting","basic wrenching"],"build_tiers":["mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler"],"year_compatibility":["2010-2024"],"tags":["wheels","beadlock","steel","alloy","bolt-pattern","backspacing","4runner"],"parts":[{"name":"Pro Comp Steel Wheel 17x9 (6x139.7, -6mm offset)","vendor":"Pro Comp","price_estimate_usd":89},{"name":"Method Race Wheels 701 Trail 17x8.5 (6x139.7, 0mm offset)","vendor":"Method Race Wheels","price_estimate_usd":175},{"name":"Fuel Off-Road Assault 17x9 (6x139.7, -12mm offset)","vendor":"Fuel Off-Road","price_estimate_usd":165},{"name":"Black Rhino Armory 17x9 (6x139.7, -12mm offset)","vendor":"Black Rhino","price_estimate_usd":155},{"name":"KMC XD Machete 17x8.5 (6x139.7)","vendor":"KMC Wheels","price_estimate_usd":145}],"citations":[{"title":"Method Race Wheels 701 Trail Product Page","url":"https://www.methodracewheels.com"},{"title":"4Runner World Wheel Fitment Guide","url":"https://www.4runner.org"}],"alternatives":["4runner-tire-fitment-33s"],"summary":"The 5th gen 4Runner shares the Tacoma's 6x139.7 bolt pattern, which opens the same wide wheel market \u2014 but the 4Runner's slightly wider track and taller body mean backspacing in the 4.75\"\u20135.25\" range for lifted builds.","description":"The 5th gen 4Runner's factory wheel is a 17x7 alloy with roughly 4.5\" of backspacing \u2014 a relatively conservative offset. On a lifted 4Runner with UCAs at 2.5\"+, moving to a 17x8.5 or 17x9 with 4.75\"\u20135.25\" backspacing (0mm to +10mm offset range) gives better tire-to-body clearance while keeping the wheel in the correct geometric position relative to the hub. Too little backspacing (high negative offset) pushes the tire outside the fender \u2014 requires flares and increases wheel bearing stress. Too much backspacing (high positive offset) tucks the tire inward toward the upper control arm and inner fender, especially on builds with UCAs.\n\nThe 4Runner's hub bore is 106.1mm \u2014 the same as the Tacoma and most Toyota 6x139.7 applications. Most aftermarket wheels ship with 108mm bore and a set of hub-centric rings to fit Toyota applications. Verify hub-centric ring inclusion before ordering, and install them \u2014 a wheel that's not seated concentrically on the hub bore will vibrate at highway speeds regardless of balance quality.\n\nMethod Race Wheels 701 Trail remains the top recommendation for lifted 4Runners. The multi-spoke design sheds mud, the 0mm offset on the 17x8.5 sits correctly for most 2.5\"\u20133\" lift builds, and the quality control is excellent. Black Rhino Armory and Fuel Assault are lower-cost alternatives with similar design philosophy \u2014 they're heavier than Method but adequate for overland and mild trail use. KMC XD Machete is a budget alloy option that works well for the price.\n\nBeadlock wheels are the correct choice for rock crawling at tire pressures of 8\u201312 PSI. At those pressures, a standard wheel can unseat the tire bead under side loading on a rock. KMC XD Machete beadlock and similar DOT-compliant designs allow legal street use while providing bead retention at low pressures. Ring bolt torque on beadlocks requires periodic checks \u2014 check every few months and after every hard trail session.","steps":["Confirm lift height and UCA configuration; select backspacing accordingly (4.75\"\u20135.25\" for 2.5\"+ lift)","Verify hub-centric ring size (106.1mm hub, 108mm bore on most aftermarket wheels)","Order wheels with hub-centric rings if not included","Have tires mounted and balanced at shop; transfer TPMS sensors from OEM wheels","Confirm TPMS sensor compatibility \u2014 4Runner TPMS sensors vary by year; verify before mounting","Install wheels; torque lug nuts to 90 ft-lb in star pattern (4Runner lug nut torque spec)","Perform TPMS relearn per owner's manual","Test clearance at full lock in both directions and at full bump before trail use","Recheck lug torque at 50 miles"],"pros":["6x139.7 bolt pattern gives access to the widest wheel selection in the off-road market","Method 701 Trail is the correct specification wheel for lifted 4Runners \u2014 proven fit and offset","Steel wheels are nearly indestructible and cost-effective for a dedicated spare","Beadlock options allow genuine low-pressure rock crawling on DOT-legal designs","KMC XD Machete delivers alloy construction at a steel wheel price point"],"cons":["Incorrect backspacing on IFS 4Runners causes inner fender contact at full compression \u2014 verify before ordering","TPMS sensors require transfer or replacement; varies by year \u2014 confirm compatibility","Low-offset wheels (-18mm and below) push tires outside fender edge \u2014 requires flares to be street legal","Beadlock ring bolts require periodic torque checks \u2014 skip this and the ring can loosen, defeating the purpose","Hub-centric rings must be installed for proper wheel seating \u2014 missing rings cause highway vibration regardless of balance quality"]}];